      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0001" n="art0001">
        <head>WM. M'CLEAN &amp; SONS,</head>
        <p>WM. M'CLEAN &amp; SONS,</p>
        <p>MONAGHAN, IRELAND. DEALERS IN FIRST-CLASS IRISH HUNTERS AND CARRIAGE HORSES AND JOB MASTERS. We beg to thank the Gentry of Liverpool and surrounding districts for their past patronage since opening our LIVERPOOL BRANCH: SANDON MEWS, 80, EGERTON ST. where owing to the increasing demand for our Speciality in High-class Carriage and Hunting Horses, and to supply this demand we have completed extensi7e alterations, and trust. by strict and personal supervision of business, to retain the confidence in the future as in the past of our numerous patrons. Also soliciting the favour of those in want of good. Sound, High-class Irish Horses, at moderate prices.to entrust us with their requirements, our sole aim being attention and entire Eatisfaction in our dealings with customers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0002" n="art0002">
        <head>PARR HALL</head>
        <p>PARR HALL</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ORNERAL MANAGER: MR. BARS. Bymntass MANAGRR : MR. BRANDON BLAKE.</p>
        <p>TWICE NIGHTLY, 7 AND 9 DOORS OPEN 6-50 AND 8-50. EARLY DOORS 6-40 AND 8-40. MONDA.Y NEXT, FEB. 25,</p>
        <p>AND DLJRPJG THR WRECK. MR. TIARNR9 PRRSIINTS THII FOLLOWING WELL-KNOWN ANGLO- iLMURIOLLN VAUDR- VILLR ENTHRTAINEIRS.</p>
        <p>FIRST APPRARANOR IN WetRRINGTON OF</p>
        <p>THE BENSONS</p>
        <p>HARMONIOUS DUBTTISTS. DANCHRS AND CAKR-WALKIIRS.</p>
        <p>VIOLET NUNN,</p>
        <p>OOMMBDIENNE.</p>
        <p>THE TW3 KAYS,</p>
        <p>VOCALISTS.</p>
        <p>D W. WATSON,</p>
        <p>THIS WORLD'S GRHATHST ONII-ARXRD CORNRTTIST AND POST HORN SOLOIST.</p>
        <p>THORNBURY, LIGHTNING CARTOONIST, VHNTRILOQUIST, OIL AND SMOKII PAINTIM</p>
        <p>SINCLAIR &amp; PARR,</p>
        <p>VERSATILII DUBTTISTS.</p>
        <p>PETER'S Premier PICTURES.</p>
        <p>3 B(XJCACIOS. In their Musical Melange, introducing</p>
        <p>SIGNOR FRANCIA, the Greatest Mandolinist on Earth.</p>
        <p>PRICES OP ADMIS SION : Back Gallery... ... 641. Barly Doors • 4d. Bide Galleries 6d. • Pit • gd, 9d. Stalls ... • is Od. le. 6d. Dress Circle Is. 6d. •.. 2s. Od. Children under 12, Half-price to all carte, Gallery excepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <p>_ " • `.A• . . ,</p>
        <p>lOUN T Y ButtOULia 011' W ArcisiNti TENDERS are invited for the supply of UNIFOR CLOTHING. HATS, &amp; to the several departments of the Corporation for the ensuing twelve months. Particulars, &amp;c„ may be obtained on and after Wednesday neat, the 27th instant, from the Borough Treasurer, Bank House, Sankey-street, and tenders, securely fastened, and endorsed " Tender for Clothing " should be sent in to the under- signed, not later than 12 o'clock noon on the 16th alareh, The Contractor wkose tender is accepted will be required to observe the recognised customs and conditions as to the rate of wages and working hours prevailing within tke District.</p>
        <p>The lowest or any tender not necessarily accepted. Municipal Offices. JOHN Fantail as r. Warrington. 22ad Feb.. 1907. Borough Treasurer.</p>
        <p>WARK INGTuN UNION. —TENDEKS. —PERSONS WILLING to Contra,ct for the snipply ot any of the undermentioned articles from Lady Diy to Micharl- mas next, to be delivered free of expense at the Workhouse or Cottage Homes situated mt Padgate at such times and in such quantities as the GJa-dians and their officers may n quire, aY rcqueste, 1 t ei.d in tenders to me on or betore Tuesday, March sth, 1907. Tenders are required for Clal and Slack Cl^gs Shoes and Slippers Crffins Brooms and Brushes</p>
        <p>Groceries and Necessaries Butchers' Meat Flour and Oatmeal Bread for Cottage Homes</p>
        <p>Drapery</p>
        <p>Leather and Shoemakers' Drugs (for 12 months) Necessaries Wsieding and Repairing Tailors' Goods Clocks (for 12 months)</p>
        <p>Uniforms fur Male Officers Wines and Spirits</p>
        <p>(for twelve months) Fish and Ice Chimney Sweeping.</p>
        <p>Separate TEnders must be sent for the Workboum and Cottage Hometi. .nd sample; of the articles required can be seen at either inoti:ution.</p>
        <p>The Fuel to be *supplied must b' according tl the specification as set forth on the ten • ei• form and quota- tions must be made for delivery either to the new siding adjoining the Workhouse or by cart. The quotation for Drugs must be as per Messrs Woolley's price list, tenderers t 9 quote what percmtage reductions they are prepared to allow. Forms of tender, and instructions to penons tender- ing, which must be strietiv compile I wits,, or any other intormation, may be obtained from the Master of the Workhouse, ard the Superintendent of the Cottage Homes respectively. The Guardians do not necessarily bind themselves to accept the lowest or any tender.</p>
        <p>By order JAMES C. SUTTON.</p>
        <p>Clcrk to the Guardians, Bewsey Chambers, Ye'nuary, 1907.</p>
      </div>
