      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>/..;tr N</head>
        <p>/..;tr N</p>
        <p>2 ONCE TRIED McDOUGALL'S Pateqt SELF-RAISING FLOUR ALWAYS USED</p>
        <p>HAVE YOU BRONCHITIS?</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Birmingham Tradesman Finds Comfort and Healing in Peps. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, r4.P. Distributes Billiard Prizes.</p>
        <p>Bronchial troubles are unfortunately so common that few people escape them. Peps, the new pine-air treat- ment for throat and chest troubles, have introduced such a unique, pleasant, and highly successful prescrip- tion for all bronchial troubles that everybody should keep Peps always handy. Take Peps at once whenever the earlier symptoms of bronchitis show themselves, and yon will be saved a world of trouble and That obstinate tickling at the back of the throat which is so familiar to most of us, causing a very i annoying dry cough, is one of the earliest symptoms of ; bronchial trouble. If neglected the trouble spreads : farther into the bronchial tubes. There is a feeling of 1 heat under the breast-bone, the cough gets deeper. and becomes painful. Tightness across the chest, especially in the morning ; difficult breathing, and much soreness on each side of the chest, under the breast-bone, and in the throat, are symptoms of advanced bronchial trouble. When the disease becomes chronic I the sufferer cannot sleep soundly, has to be propped up in bed to get breath, and is constantly coughing night and day. Asthma often accompanies bronchitis; and the heart becomes affected for lack of sufficient oxygen. Peps brings the pure healing breath of the pine forest to the rescue, enabling the sufferer to inhale the balsamic pine-essences direct into the bronchial tubes as the little tablets dissolve in the mouth. Peps con- tain no opium, chloral, cocaine, bromides, or any other narcotics or sedatives; but they never fail to bring instant and natural relief in the most obstinate cases. Mr. Charles Miller, a well-known furniture and antique dealer, of 102, 104 and 108, High street, Erdington, Birmingham, writes:—" I feel it my duty to inform you of the great relief I have obtained through taking Peps. I suffered for four years with bronchitis, but could get no lasting relief till I tried Peps. The first box worked wonders, and Tam delighted to tell you that lain now thoroughly cured. lam willing to give fall proof of mx statement to any suffer3r from bronchitis, and I hope that many may profit by my experience, and take Peps as I did. Keep Peps always by your bedside, and that dry, tickling cough which keeps you awake at nights, and disturbs the rest of everybody in the house, will dis- appear as the pleasant little tablets dissolve on your tongue, bringing comfort andrefreshing sleep. Peps are sold by all chemists and storm at is. lid. and 2s. 9d. per box, or may be had post free at the same prices from The Peps Pastile Co., Carlton Hill, Leeds, or C. E. Falford, Ltd., Leeds. See that the registered name— Peps—is on every box you buy.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 11. Crosfield. M. 13., visited the Liberal Club on Saturday evening and presented the prizes gained in the Christmas billiard handicap. There was a large attendance of members and Mr. Crosfield was greeted with the warmest enthusiasm. As president of the club, he has taken a keen interest in its welfare and his gift of the handsome prizes handed to the successful competitors on Saturday was only one more evidence of the Borough tlember's desire to encourage wholesome recreation. Alderman Tinni on, as chairman of the committee, presided and was sup- ported by Mr. C. E. Parker (president of the Liberal Association), Councillors Evans and Wilkinson, Mr. J. Worrall and Mr. T. Pemberton (hon. secretary). . .</p>
        <p>• The CHAIRMAN said he was exceedingly pleased to welcome Mr. Crosaeld and he 'could only express regret that circumstances had prevented his taking part in similar social gatherings since his election. The mem- bers of the club owed a great deal to Mr. Crosfield. He had been a captain over them and he (the speaker) did not hesitate to say that during Mr. Crosaeld's presidency, the club had done more than any other department of Liberal activity in Warrington to keep alive Liberal sentiments in Warrington. (Applause.) Some people had said that Mr. Crostield paid too much attention to the social part of the progress of the club. He (Alderman Tinnion) distinctly denied it. Mr. Crostleld had done a great deal of work for the Liberal party but there was no part which had borne better fruit than his work on behalf of the Liberal Club. (Applause.) They cordially thanked him for his past services and hoped they would have him as their presi- dent for many years to crime. (Applause). Mr. CROSFIELD then presented the prizes to the following:-1, marble striking clock and pair of bronzes, Mr. W. Warburton • 2, case of cutlery, Ur. J. W. Plinston ; 3, rose bowl, Mr. J. Johnson; 4, six bottla dinner cruet, Mr. M. Tobin; 5, Westminster cabinet spoons and cutlery, Mr. W. Ford; 6, Wedgwood biscuit jar, Mr. J. Gorst ; 7, silver plated tea pot, Mr. R. Woods; 8, case of carvers (buckhorn), Mr. W. H. Dutton, jun.; 9, salad bowl and servers, Mr. T. Locker; 10, silver plated sardine dish, Mr. J. Sankey; 11, case of carvers, Mr. F. Parr; 12. silver plated breakfast cruet, Mr. C. H. Hibbert. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM ATHERTON.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. WILLIAM ATHERTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington has lost another of its worthiest citizens by the death of Mr. William Atherton, which occurred on Friday evening at his residence, Folly Lane. The deceased gentleman who was in his 72nd year. Had been in a delicate state of health for about three years and had been confined to the house for several months by a severe attack of gout, to which he succumbed. A native of Warrington, he was apprenticed as a lad at the old forge in Dallam-lane, in the pattern department,and rose step by step until he was appointed foreman of the Pattern shops, and subsequently to the responsible post of manager of the engineering department. He was greatly respected by employers and employed, and on his retirement in 1905 after a strenuous career was the recipient of the good wishes of all at Dallam. He vgas succeeded on his retirement by Mr. Thomas Atherton one of his sons. The deceased gentleman, who leaves three sons and one daughter, was of a most unassuming disposition. He never took any active part in local matters, devoting most of his time to his duties at the works, in which he took a very deep interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FUNEBAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNEBAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Cemetery at one o'clock on Tuesday afternoon. The procession was headed by about 150 workmen. The principal mourners were : First carriage: Dr. Smith, Mr. S. Wallhead, and the Rev, NV. Bracecamp. Second carriage : Messrs. Jonathan Atherton (brother), W. H. Atherton (son), T. Atherton (son), and Master Willie Atherton (grandson).' Third carriage: Messrs. J. Houghton, C. Bunch, and W. G. Lecomber. Fourth carriage: Aldeimen Evans, Mr. T. Partridge, Councillor Wilkinson, and Mr. R. Chadwick. Fifth carriage: Messrs. Joseph Bunch, W. Bunch, J. Cowdell, and Joseph Wilkinson. Sixth carriage: Messrs. S. J. Hewitt, J. Hankinson, J. Holden, and George Mackey. Seventh carriage: Messrs. G. L. Brimelowar. Massey, T. Gregory, and J. Gibson. Eighth carriage: Messrs. T. Davies, Joseph Smith, S. Barlow, and J. W. Johnston. Ninth carriage: Messrs. J. Rigby, J. Clark, J. Hewitt, and J. Clegg (old employes of the Pearson and Knowles Company). Among others present were Messrs. W. H. Bleckly, Herbert S. Bleckley, Arthur Bleckly, John Bleckly, jun., and Wilfred Blackly, jun. His Worship the Mayor was unable to be present owing to absence from town, and Mr. Beck and Mr. James Dodds by indisposition. There was a larga attendance of Freemasons, including Bros. Beswick, W.M., T. Healing, S.W., T. Hill, P.M., treasurer; E. Rose, P.M., P.Pr.D.G.D.C., secretary ; R. T. Smith, P,M., Rev. B. Percival, chaplain; J. R. Griffiths, P.M.. J Birchall, S.D., R. Chadwick, J.D., A. G. Webster, P.M., S. Wellhead, P.M., P.Pr.G.R., S. Wakefield, T. F. W. Dixon, Joseph Taylor and W. Price, S.S. (" Gilbert Greenall" Lodge, 1250), and Bros. H. F. Greenough, P.M. 148, P.P.G.5.8., and C. Bunch, 2651. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. W. Bracecamp, -vicar of St. Paul's. The coffin, which was of fumed oak, with brass mountings, contained the following inscription on a brass plate :—" Wiliam Atherton, born September 30, 1835 ; died 15th February, 1907." The bearers were the following foremen :—Messrs. Ellwell, Lee, Hornby, Gilmore, Scott, and Rosterne.</p>
        <p>THE WREATHS. The following ladies and gentlemen sent wreaths : The directors of the Pearson and Knowles Co., Ltd., Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Bieckly, Mr. and Mrs. H. S. Bleckly, Mr. Arthur S. Bleakly, Mr. John Blackly, jun., Mr. and Mrs. Wilfred BlecklV, Mr. Ed. Beck, Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds, Mr. arid Mrs. C. E. Bunch, Capt. and Mrs. Savage, Mrs. and Mrs. John Holden and family, Miss Atherton (Froghall-lane), Mr. S. Wakefield, Mr. and Mrs. J. Bailey, Mrs. James Allen and family, Mr. and Mrs. James Bowcock, Mr. and Mrs. Thos. F. W. Dixon, Mr. and Mrs. Ed. Massey, Mr. and Mrs. H. F. Greenough, the moulders at Dallam Forge, Mr. and Mrs. Clegg and family, Mr. and Mrs. Clive Mr. and Mrs. John Gibson, Mr. and Mrs. G. L. Brimelow, the Drawing office staff at Dallam, Forge, the office staff of the Engineering Department at Dallam Forge, Mr. Jonathan Atherton and family Mrs. Mackey and family, Mr. and Mis. T. Partridge, Mrs. S. Winks, Mrs. and Miss Done, Nurse Howard, Mr. and Mrs. Robert Chadwick, Mr. S. J. Hewitt," His daughter Lilies," Mr. and Mrs. Tom Atherton, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Atherton, "his grandson Willie," " his grandchildren, Katie, Torn, and George," Tom and Nellie Atherton, Mr. and Mrs. J. Richardson (Spring- field-street), workmen of the Engineering Department at Dallarn Forge, Mr. and Hrs. John Hankinson, Mr.and Mrs. W. Rogers, Mr. T. C,rtntl3r. ‘Er.ilatwt S.nith, Bewsey Office Staff, and Mr. and Mrs. J. Bunch. The whole of the funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. H. F. Greenough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LIVERPOOL P3LISHER'S PERIL, Zam-Buk Cures Poisoned and Ulcerated Arms.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL P3LISHER'S PERIL, Zam-Buk Cures Poisoned and Ulcerated Arms.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph H. Jones, of 2, Raglan Terrace, Alm'. street, Evertor', Liverpool, has given an account of his remarkable cure by Zatn-Buk to a Liverpool reportor. "About seven or eight years ago," said Mr. Jones, "I was working at a new cafe in the city, when my fingers got covered with blisters in consequence of a strong polish I had been using. These blisters became very irritable, and in a short time developed into ulcers,which 'caused me to seek medical attention. For several years I was taking medicine but I never seemed to properly recover. Shortly after Christmas the affection spread to my arms and legs and I was quite unable to work and had to lay up. My lingers were swollen stiff, and were all out of shape, whilst my arms had to be bandaged twice a day, and I was helpless as a child. My wife had to wash, dress and feed me, and I settled down as a con- firmed invalid.</p>
        <p>"About this time my wife had been using 'Lam-Buk for her chapped hands, and had received such benefit that she prevailed on me to give it a trial. I did so, with a result that can only be described as magical. With absolute truth I may say that from the very first I derived benefit from its application, and continuing with the treatment at the end of a fortnight I was com- pletely cured. The swellings had gone, the stiffne,m vanished, and the sore places all healed up. I attribute this entirely to the soothing and curative powers of Zam-Buk, and when I think of the nights of sleepless- lessness I had suffered, of the aptly I endured so long it seems incredible that the disease has now left me. I am now in splendid condition. We have also found Zam-Buk very benticial for the cuts and bruises of the children, and in my opinion it should be in everybody's house. I shall take care that lam never without it as long as I live." The healing, soothing and antiseptic qualties of Zam- Buk are invaluable for all forms of skin disease and injuries. Every Warrington household should take a leaf out -of Mr. Jones' book and keep a box always handy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WIDNES DOCK FATALITY.</head>
        <p>WIDNES DOCK FATALITY.</p>
        <p>- - - At West Bank Dock, Widnes, on Tuesday afternoon, a man named John Perro,said to be a native of St. Helens living on the flat Richard, belonging to Liverpool, met with an accident which terminated fatally. He was pulling his flat along the dock wall underneath a coal tip when he fell overboard between the wall and the fiat side. He was taken out of the water and was con- veyed to the hospital, but expired on the way.</p>
        <p>In handing the first prize to Mr. •Warburton, Mr. Crosfleld said that gentleman was one of the oldest if not the oldest member of the club. (Applause.) He belonged to it before they came into those premises, when the club was in Market-street. They all congratu- lated him very much on winning what was really one of the most handsome prizes it had ever been his (Mr. Crosfleld's) pleasure to present to any successful com- petitor at bowls or billiards. (Applause.) Having distributed all the priz.ls, Mr. CROSFIELD said he wished to thank Alderman Tinnion, as he had thankedlhica many a time before, for the kind things he had said about him. Ifiieferance to what little he had been able to do for the Liberal Club, he knew perfectly well how little it was, but he could at least endorse one remark Alderman Tinnion made,and that was that what- ever his position might be in the old town, he hoped he would always be able to some small extent to farther the interests of that club with which those who had gone before him were connected and whose walls bore the portraits of many old members of the Liberal party whom they were always proud to remember. (Applause) There were other groat local organizations that made i for progress, but t was a grand thing to have a good club with plenty of good fellowship which might be a rallying-point where they could meet together and exchange opinions. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that club would always as years went on play its part both on the social and political side of affairs. (Applause.) Speaking there, he felt he was on familiar ground. On Monday he would have the opportunity and the honour of addressing his constituents in refer- ence to the work of Parliament during the past year, of which he felt, as he expected erery Liberal present felt pretty proud. (Hear, hear.) He was ready also to answer for his own short-comings and to undergo, he presumed, that purifying ordeal which was the lot of most politicians and which their Scotch friends—always clever—described as heckling. (Laughter.) That night, however, while on that familiar ground and amougst friends, he would like to tell them with a frankness that, he thought, had been known in other members of his family who had gone before him, of the difficulties under which he had laboured for the last twelve months. He had quoted before a saying of Byron that there was one subject on which all men could be eloquent and very few agreeable, and that was themselves. (Laughter), Unfortunately it sometimes, and perhaps rather too often, fell to the lot of politicians to talk a little bit about themselves, and he thought it was better that, even at the risk of boring them, he should explain certain matters. Mr. Crosfield then referred to his strenuous business life and the strain upon his health during the fifteen years following 1834, when the chief responsibility in the concern at Bank Quay was thrown upon him and he had to replace the decaying old- fashioned trade with a new class of business. When he (Mr. Crosaeld) undertook to fight the battle in Warrington he had every reason to think he would be clear of business but, as events turned out, he had two or three years of continuous business and politics, and excepting a month abroad now and again, he never had time to touch a golf club from year's end to year's end, and they knew that golf was one of his hobbies. (Laughter.) The long and short of it was that during a good part of last session his health was not all that one could desire and it had grieved him very much that owing to pressure of business and to the fact that his health had suffered to some extent, he bad not been able to attend as many meetings in Warrington as he could wish to have done. He had heard in years gone by complaints, and legitimate one's too, of the lack of political instruction they had had in Warrington. All he could say was that, unable to take his share as much as he could have wished, they had at any rate, had able speakers to address them and he thought his constituents had had good opportunities for that political instruction which he trusted would never be wanting in the future to the progressive party of War- rington.. Returning to the social side of the club, Mr. Crosfleld said that many a time he bad spoken in favour of those outdoor and indoor games in which he took a great interest and always intended to—it ran in the blood of his family to some extent—and which be trusted would conduce to the prosperity of the club. (Applause) Referring to the value of games as a means to an end, Mr. Crosfleld said that just as they brought classes together in our own country, so they brought nation and nation together. Assuring the members of his continued interest in the club and in the progress of the town, Mr. Crosfleld said that whatever might happen in the future, Liberalism had not been to him a stepping stone to Parliament. He had read in the newspapers what people had been kind enough to ' say—that Parliament was the means for grinding one's own axe and putting a few pounds into one's pocket. He could assure them from his own experience that it was quite the other way. (Laughter.) Whatever his defici- encies might he, there was no axe of any kind that he had to grind there. (Applause.) fie had no patience whatever with people who depreciated the House of Commons. On the contrary, anyone like himself going there without any previous special training for politics found it the noblest and most admirable political work- shop in the world. (Applause.) Macaulay was once described as a man whose brain was like the deans of the British IMuseurn, and if they looked at some of the great workers in the House of Commons and saw how even they were obliged to specialise, how their great brain capacity was inadequate to all the work that went on there' they began to •feel a little respect for that great Chamber—the leader of our own democracy which in turn ought to be the leader of the other democracies in the world. (Applause). Mr. WORItALL moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Cros- I field for his presence and for again presenting the prizes. No club, he said, ever had a more popular president or one who took a keener interest in its work than Mr. Crosfleld. (Applause.) Councillor Wit,TuNsoa; seconded and assured M. Crosfleld that his kindness was fully appreciated by the mribers.</p>
        <p>Mr. -CROSPIELD briefly responded. He was, he said, but a humble soldier in the great army in the House which was carrying on a splendid work and was making hiBtorY—a Government which was going to be memor- able in the annals of our country and was, in fact, the healthiest, soundest, and most vigorous development. of democracy that any man could wish to see in his life- time. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKEa moved a vote of thanks to Alder- man Tinaion and paid a tribute to his loyality to the club. He (tr. Parker) recognised as much as anyone the good work the club was doingand the valuable asset it was in connection with the last Parliamentary con- test. Mr. Parker spoke of the enthusiasm throughout the party which led to such a magnificent victory and said that now they had captured the seat they meant to retain it. (Applause.) Councillor EVANS seconded the motion which w.is heartily passed.</p>
        <p>Warrington Rough Sent to Gaol. A man named Joseph Abraham, of Dial-street, Warrington, appeared in the dock at the Warrington Police Court, on Monday, charged with being drunk and disorderly, and further with an aggravated assault upon Hannah Needham. It was stated that c unplain- ant lived with the prisgner, but a week ago she left him. On Saturday night, as she was proceeding along Dial-street,he sprang upon her from behind, threw her on the ground, and kicked her several times in a savage manner about the head and body. Prisoner was taken into custody, and on the way to the police station he said if he could get at her again he would kick her to death. The magistrates committed him to prison for one month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
