      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 23, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 23, 1907</p>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>INCREASED RATE SANCTIONED.</p>
        <p>The Re-naming of People's College.</p>
        <p>An increase of 2d. in the rate required for educational purposes was sanctioned by the Warrington Education Committee which at its meeting on Monday evening had under consideration,the estimates for the ensuing year. Alderman Bennett presided and there were also present: Councillor' Fulton-Smith (deputy-chairman), Aldermen Bolton, Evans, and Tinnion, Councillors Barton, Ball, Clegg, Ellison, Evans, Dr. Joseph, Lord, Sankey, Steel, Toole, Wilkinson, Revs. Canon Stevenson, J. Davies, John Yonge and N. Wilson, Messrs. C. Sawkins Burgeis, T. P. Bradshaw, It. T. Fairclough, A. J. Jolley, H. Kingdoo, C. E. Parker, E. Plinston, H. R Aorta, C. H. Unsvvorth, G. Walker; Mr. J. Moore Murray (director), Mr. H. B. Roberts (assistant director), Mr. John Fairhurst (treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LATE DR. GORNALL.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. GORNALL.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRNIAN said he had a painful duty to perform and that was to move a resolution with reference to the death of Dr. Gornall, medical officer of health, express- ing the regret of the committee at the loss they had sustained and conveying to the widow and family deep sympathy in their bereavement. It was not, said Alder- man Bennett, necessary to say much about the Doctor because ample tributes had already been paid to his unselfishness, his ability, and his services in various capacities to the town. It was almost impossible to exaggerate those services. The late Doctor riad fought against smoke, filth, fever, disease in all its forms, and death, too, but, unfortunately, in the fight he had suc- cumbed himself. He had acted as Medical Orricer to the Education Committee, and his work in that respect , had been extremely onerous and had been discharged with that ability and great conscientiousness which characterised the whole of his duties. (Hear, hear.) Councillor FULTON-SMITH seconded, and said the tributes which had been paid to the deceased gentle- man had been not only fitting but heartfelt. The greatest tribute that could be offered to his memory would be to silently pass the vote knowing that they had lost a good man, an excellent citizen and an admir- able officer. The motion was carried unanimously. COST OF EDUCATION. THE ESTIMATES APPROVED.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. .1. JOLLEY, as chairman of the Finance Corn- mittee.then moved the adoption of the estimates for the coming year, which involve a rate for educational pur- poses of about is. 4id. as comparel with is. 3d. last year. The following summary for 1907-8 shows the estimated expenditure of each department :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PUBLIC</head>
        <p>PUBLIC</p>
