        <p>(19OG-7)-214732 19 4 Mr. JOLLEY said' ~it would have been more satisfactory if they could have had the full figures, but, as the estimates had to be closed before the end of the finan- cial year, it was impossible. The utmost attention had however, been given to ascertain what the probable outlay would have been during the ensuing weeks.(Hear hear.) Mr. Jolley then gave exhaustive comparisons with regard to many of the items and explained why certain increases had taken 'place. Alluding to the Technical School, he said that Warrington had in it a first class building with a first class equipment of which the ratepayers might be justly proud, and none the less so since it was nota product of posthumous benevolence nor one of those crumbs of conscience which at irregular intervals fell from multi-millionaires' tables' but had been provided by themselves for themselves. Might be further added that the thanks of the town were due to Mr. Robson and his staff? In connection with both the Secondary School and the technical classes there had been an energy and enthusiasm which had deserved and secured a large measure of success, and which would in the future bear even better fruit. In view of the some- what carping criticism which had occasionally been directed against this department of their work he wanted to say as plainly as possible that class lists and grant payments were not the only or the highest tests of educational efficiency, hut that whether measured by these or by less sordid standards higher eiucation in Warrington was not found wanting. He was only voicing the sentiment of competent critics when he said on their behalf that they highly appreciated the work</p>
