        <p>Beware of Substitutes; ask for " OWBRIDGE " when buying Lung Tone and TAKE NO OTHER.</p>
        <p>Prepared by 4 W. T. OWBRIDGE, Ltd., Hull. Sold everywhere in bottles at is. lid., 2s. 9d., 4s. 6d., and us.</p>
        <p>approved and adopted. He was surprised that at the meeting of the School Management Committee anyone should have thought it necessary to bring forward and also that there should be an insistence on the name of " Brewtnall." It was said that. it was he who laid the foundation of the school and gave character to it and to many of the pupils, and also that the managers had been a close corporation and that it was probably necessary to administer them a lesson. As a schoolmaster he ( Alderman Tinnion) was happy to say he did not know Mr. Brevrtnall but he understood he was not connected with theArpley-street Schools and he was not the first master either.Aiderman Tinnion then briefly traced the history of the school pointing out that the first master was Mr. Wright and that the curriculum of the school in the old days was such as to make it a semi-college and not simply an elementary school. It had bean suggested that the management was a close corporation. But that was not so. The present board consisted of a Churchman. a Methodist, Quaker, a Congregationalist, and if they had a Roman Catholic they would have been the most representative body in the town or district. Alderman Tinuion read the names of a large number of gentletnen who had been connected with the school. The managers he said felt very strongly about the matter. If it was desirable to give any name at all of distinctive charac- ter the master who should be honoured was the man who had done more than any other man, Mr. Charles Twiss. (Hear, hear.) But the opinion was that it was not desirable to give anypersonal name and in this con- nection Alderman Tinnion jocularly remarked that he did not know why tin Bolton schools had been so named.</p>
        <p>The DEPUTY-SIASOR seconded the amendment, and said he was once a scholar of the school when it was known as " Stubb's School" although Mr. Brewtnall was the headmaster. He thought the wish of the managers ought to be acceded to, as they were the people who had to work the school. Mr. ROBERTS said he expected to be beaten, but not without a kick. (Laughter.) He wished to correct one or two misstatements made by Alderman Tinnion. He (Kr. Roberts) made a suggestion at the last meeting that the management of the school had been in the past a close corporation. He repeated that state- ment. El e was not making any reference to the present managers but to past history. He had stated a fact that, with the exception of Mr. Linnmus Greening, during the whole of the GO years not one of the old scholars had ever been upon the management. That was evidence of the closeness of the management—it was a self-elected committee from their own particular clique. In Mr. Brewtnall's time, the school made its name, it reached the height of its fame as an elementary school, and the foundation of the school in Arpley-street was laid by one of the best schoolmasters the town ever saw. They had recently named two schools, one after the first Mayor and in regard to the second, Alderthan Tinnion had very properly questioned why on earth the school should be called after the last Mayor. (Laugh- ter.) In all his experience he had never had a greater difficulty than he had in that case to reconcile the name or to find a radical, sensible, respectable reason for giving it, but having admitted the practice of calling</p>
