        <p>for Lancashire—which represented employers and men —was concerned there was no rule to prevent the employment of bricksetters' labourers under the con- ditions they were employed under in regard to that school. Mr. Davenport complied to the full with the conditions which trades unions imposed. Mr. PLENSTON : If that question comes up I shall be in a position to prove that Mr. Roberts is wrong in the assertion he has made to-night. The CFINIRM AS congratulated Alderman Bolton on the economy which had been exercised, and said he hoped artistic effect and red etliciency had not been sacrificed. The DEPUTY-AA von said they did not wish any friction to exist lietween mksters and workmen. They preferred everything, to be done in a satisfactory manner to suit trades unionists and employers. If they did not pull together they would all lose money. The minutes were then agreed to.</p>
