      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SEAWEED IN KIDNEY DISEASES.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED IN KIDNEY DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Dr. James Walker. Kidney and Urinary Specialist, New York, says :—"I -never treat a case where the kidneys, urinary organs or stomach are involved without seaweed. I regard it as indispensable." k The species of seaweed used by Dr. Walker is the same as that contained in Veno's Seaweed Tonic. Veno's Seaweed Tonic is used by many prominent doctors in all forms of kidney and urinary diseases because of its great strengthening, healing and purifying effect. It cures nephritis, gright's disease, uric acid troubles, weakness, dropsy and backache; especially successful in constipation. Pleasant to take. Cares permanently. No return.</p>
        <p>Ask for Veno's Seaweed Tonic, at chemists every- where, one and three-halfpence and two-and-nine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>TEIEFTs AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>TEIEFTs AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes on Tuesday Peter Grady (27), labourer, who pleaded guilty to stealing 741 b. of lead. the propriety of the United Alkali Company, at Widnes, between January Bth and 12th. was sent to gaol for four months.—William Booth (32), labourei., was found guilty of having, on the 23th January, stolen a number, of small articles from the shed of Mr. Owen Davies, of Widnes. He was committed to gaol for three months with bard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ANOTHER WARRINGTON MAN.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER WARRINGTON MAN.</p>
        <p>It is pleasant to hear that a St. Helens resident has met with most welcome good fortune. There are many of us here who suffer from kidney trouble of ono kind or another, backache, rheumatism, dropsy or gravel, 80 that this news of a sure means of cure is truly welcome. But we are not surprised, such cures by Doan's Bsck- ache Kidney Pills occur every day. Mr. William H. Taylor, 2, Park-cottages. City-road, St. Helens, says :—" I have no objection to the facts of my case being published because I am grateful for the good Doan's backache kidney pills have done me. "I suffered greatly from kidney complaint: I had severo pains in my back,and my symptoms corresponded exactly with what I read in one of Doan's booklets. My hands and feet were as cold as clay, I had a nasty taste in my mouth, Ind there were urinary disorders. "I was told I had acute kidney disease, and though I was treated by several doctors, I got no better until I used Doan's backache kidney pills. I have taken these pills from time to time, ani have received great benefit from them. I have spoken to many people about Doan's pills. (Signed) W. H. TAYLOR.'" Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence par box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-streets, Oxford-street, London, %V. You are sure to get the right medicine if you ask distinctly for Doan's.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that there was nothing absolutely definite so long as the works had not been commenced. IHe did not know whether any penalties were attached. Mr. Baxter asked whether in view of the imminence of old age pensions it would be wise to proceed with the extension of the Workhouse. They might spend £2,000 on the work and then find themselves faced with the fact that the buildings were not wanted. He had followed the discussions in Parliament and the Labour members who seemed to rule had ordered that this question should be considered during the present session and if they wanted it considered it would be considered and successfully too. Mr. Douglas: We should do as little as we can. Mr. Baxter: I think we should defer any extension. Mr. Woodward said he was of opinion that old age pensions were sure to come. The condition of the country at the present moment was such that which- ever political party was in power the outcome of the agitation would be old age pensions and in view of that fact it would be a great mistake to go any further with the extension of the Workhouse. (Hear,hear.) If they took an effective retrospective view of Poor Law administration over many years it would be found that establishment charges for such places as those were so heavy that they took a large portion of the money required for the support of the poor. In view of that and of the anticipated provision by Parliament it would be a mistake at present to enlarge the Workhouse. Mr. Spruce said the pressing needs were the Niaternity Ward and the Nurses' Home. The Board would remem- ber that he took exception to the excessive cost of the Nurses' Home when the report was presented. The cost now exceeded what was anticipated by some few hundred pounds but the provision of a Maternity Ward was urgent, and he did not see how they could wait. The House was very crowded. There were 14 more inmates than in the corresponding period of last year. If, however, the work could be deferred he would sup- port a resolution to that effect. Mr. Hough pointed out that some of the alterations had been brought about by the suggestion of the Local Government Board Inspector, and certain costs had already been incurred. If anything were done it ought to be done by notice of motion. Mr. Linaker said he understood that tenders had been accepted upon plans passed by the Local Government Board. That being so it was not likely that the Local Government Board would give their sanction for the work to be postponed. He had not been a keen advo- cate for extension, and he felt that the matter had been forced upon them. They had that afternoon issued certain orders to the House, and he understood that they had not the accommodation to meet those orders. He did not think Old Age Pensions were so near as to justify them in going back upon what they had done. Having accdpted a tendor for the work he did not see how they could recede from their position. They were on the horns of a dilemma.</p>
        <p>Mr. Cunnah urged that the maternity ward and nurses' borne were absolutely necessary. Some years ago they were refused nurses on the ground that they had not sufficient accommodation for the nurses. Mr. Baxter said there was no doubt that the maternity ward and nurses' home were required. His reason for introducing the question was that they were increasing the accommodation for paupers. He was still of opinion that before long the workhouses would not be required to be as big at present. The Chairman said he was not sttistled that they were not spending more money than was absolutely necessary. He understood that they provided for a suite of committee rooms.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward thought the last remark Wag an unfortunate one. They were not p.oviding a suite of committee rooms. They were merely adapting certain existing _rooms as committee rooms.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker did not see that anything could be done as they had let the contract. The question was allowed to drop and it was decided to apply to the Local Government Board for permission to borrow money for the work.</p>
        <p>M.P., urged the members to be true to one another,and Ito trade unionism, and he was accorded a hearty vote of thanks. Bro. W. Davies,president of the branch presided, and said the chief item on the programme was a pre- sentation claim of £lOO, to a brother who,unfortunately had met withan accident and lost an eye. Other items of interest should have been the presentation of emblems to the Secretary, Bro. H. S. Turner, and Bros. F. and A. Jolley, past officers of the branch, but, unfor- tunately, the emblems had not arrived from the head office. Arrangements would be made later to present them. Bro. S. Cordingley, a superannuated member, presented the bonus claim of £lOO to Bro. Edward Davies, of 3, Hope-street, who, while working at the Great Western Railway works at Swindon met with an accident which resulted in the loss of his left eye, and rendered him unable to follow his oocbpation as a boil- maker. Bro. J. Cordingley, in presenting the cheque, spoke of the many advantages a man had by being in a trade society, Be urged them to keep their contribn- tioas straight as they never knew what misfortune might overtake them. Bro. E. Davies suitably replied. During the evening a musical programme was gone through. .</p>
      </div>
