      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES BURGLAR'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>During Saturday night the premises of Mr. Murphy, 18, Oxford-ftreet, Widnes, were entered, and 12s. 6d. in money and two watches were taken. It appears that Mr. Murphy was out working on the night shift, and his wife, being of a nervous ditposition, west to sleep with her mother a few doors higher up the street. The housebreaker, finding the doors strongly barred, broke a pane of glass and opened the catch. After a general survey of the house, which he had entirely to himself, he decamped with the above articles, leaving behind him on the table a note which read," You should marry a woman who will stop at home and look after the house."</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. JOHN VALENTINE, WIDNES. On Saturday afternoon the mortal remains of the late Mr. John Valentine, of Woodcrofts, Halton View, Widnes, for many years the Widnes representative of Messrs. Greenall, IVhitley and Co., were laid to rest in Farnworth Churchyard, amid many 'manifestations of public sympathy. The cortege left the residence of deceased at a quarter past twelve o'clock, and proceeded to St. Ambrose's Church, where the Rev. H. WHiscoke and the Rev. E. Traffbrd conducted the funeral service. A long procession of forty carriages followed the hearse to Farnworth. where the last rites were performed by the Rev. H. P. Hiscoke. The carriers were Messrs. H. Percy, J. Rothwell, R. Eden, 'l'. Gibbons' W. Rigby and E. Mason. -Amongst those present were Aldermen Neil, and Wareing, Councillors Neil, Bullough, Welding, Winfield, Brennan, Dr. Brunner (medical officer), the Rev. J. W. Williams and others.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIXNS. ALTERATIONS TO THE WORKEIOUSE.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present : Alderman Norman (presiding), Messrs. T. Baxter, T. Egerton-Warburton, T. Wright, P. Collins, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, J. Bolshaw, J. Hallows, C. Roylance, J. Spruce, J. Whitley, H. Eyes, W. Weir, 1. Whitlow, T. Antwis, H. M. Davies, G. Pilling, J. H. Davies, H. Mawdsley, C. Horton, E. Bretherton, P. Moundeld, J. Douglas, W. Harrison, A. Broome, W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), W 4 R. de Prez, T. Cook and J. Pearson (relieving officers) and J. Cunnah (master.) The Guardians of Chesterfield Union wrote enclosing copy of the following resolution, in the hope that the proposals contained would be regarded with favour "That in the opinion of this Board naval and army pensioners should be placed upon the same footing as members of Friendly Societies, if they are compelled to apply for out-door relief, and that no portion of their pension up to and including the sum of ss. per week shall be taken into account by Boards of Guardians when granting out-door relief; and that it be an instruc- tion to the Clerk to approach local members of Parlia- ment with the view of their taking up and introducing into the House of Commons financial measures on lines in that direction." It was decided to support the resolution. . . _</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the Pontypool Guardians had for- warded a resolution demanding legislation for the removal of the anomaly set up by the recent ruling af the Court of the King's Bench that a married woman with ample separate estate was not liable to contribute towards the maintenance of her parents or parent. Mr. Linaker : As the women want the vote let them take the responsibility. I move we support it. This course was adopted. _ _ WORKHOUSE ALTERATIONS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter asked if the Board had gone so far in the extension to the Workhouse that they could not withdraw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>COST OF OUT RELIEF.</head>
        <p>COST OF OUT RELIEF.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward reported payment in out-relief of £GS to Mr. Cook, 25) to Mr. de Prez and £27i to Mr. Pearson</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN MAN SIGNED ON FOR</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Torn Watson, the Liverpool's secretary, on Saturday signed on Bowyer Bustling, inside right of the Hades- town Club. He is a smart forward and a good shot.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF THE REV. A. AINLEY.</p>
        <p>We learn that the Rev. A. Ainley has tendered his resignation as curate of St. John's parish, where he has made himself popular, as the" Young Men's Friend." MR. SEDDON, M.P., ON THE HOUSE OF LORDS</p>
        <p>Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P., took part in the debate on the House of Lords in Parliament on Monday. Remark- ing on the new-born zeal of the Tories for social reform, he reminded them that it would cost money. Would they be prepared to support a graduated income tax? He hoped the Chancellor of the Exchequer would keep the Opposition up to their expressions of goodwill for social reform. For himself, he preferred to judge them by their deed's. The present Government had given some indication of their desire for social reform. They last session adopted the Feeding of School Children Bill. The Tory Party day after day fought that Bill on the ground that it was Socialism. He supposed that because it was Socialism it could not be social I reform. When the Bill went to the Upper House ;it was mutilated by the Lords. Yet they were told the Tory party was anxious for social reform! He supposed they would treat old age pensions lin the same way. They -would no doubt object that they had not the money. But the resources of taxation were not exhausted. Even without tariff reform the ' enormous incomes which were being drawn at the present time ought to yield more than enough to pro- vide old age pensions. He hoped that if necessary the I Chancellor of the Exchequer would so widen the basis I of taxation that both this reform and the land question would be dealt with in order that relief might be given to the veterans of industry and the victims of over- crowding. The Lords had by their action on the Trades , Disputes Bill shown that they were a cowardly body who had outlived their usefulness, and the sooner they were abolished the better would it be for the people of this country.</p>
        <p>I BOILER MAKERS' 62,ND ANNIVERSARY.' I The Newton-le-Willows Branch of the Boiler Makers Society met to celebrate their 62nd anniversary at their head quarters, the Sunbeam Inn, on Saturday. A sub- stantial knife and fork tea was served by the host and hostess,Mr. and Mrs. Wainwright. The visitors included Dr. Valentine, Bro. W. McElvoy (chairman of the Man- chester District Committee), and Bro. J. W. Lovell (district auditor). During the evening, Mr. J. A. Seddon</p>
        <p>- A JUVENILE SHOPBREAKER. A small lad named Frank Fitchett pleaded guilty at Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday last week to being in the shop of John Cunliffe, 82, Blackbrook-road, Haydock, for an unlawful purpose and stealing 3d. and some clothing, the property of John Cunliffe, carpenter and grocer.—lt appeared that on the 3rd of January, Mr.. Cunliffe,going downstairs in the morning,found the back door open and the clothing and money were missed, the clothing being afterwards found hidden under some wood. On the 7th February the boy was found hiding in the shop ; he was given into custody and admitted thrill he was the thief in January.—The boy's mother was in Court and was told he would be bound over to come up for judgment if called upon. CROSS-CHARGES AND HARD SWEARING. John Vogles was summoned at the Newton Petty Sessions last Thursday for using threatening language to Campbell Russell on the night of Saturday, February 2nd, and also with having been drunk and disorderly in Queen-street, Earlestown, and with assaulting Robert Black.—There was a cross-summons in which Vogles charged Russell witk assaulting him.—Mr. J. W. Knowles, appearing for Russell, who is a racing man living in Crow Lane East, Newton, said the pause of action began at Bank Quay Station. Warrington, when Russell was returning home to Newton_ with a friend named Black. Vogles followed him about with very foul and threatening language, wanted to fight him for a liver, and did all he could to provoke him to fight. The stationmaster sent for the police but the train left before they arrived. When they got to Earlestown Vogles followed Russell and Black along the subway and the street. Russell ran to get out of his way and complained to two policemen whom he met. They locked Vogles up, and he was afterwards bailed out. It was not the first time he had caused Mr. Russell annoyance as would be shown in evidence.— Plaintiff gave evidence agreeing with Mr. Knowles's opening, and in answer to Mr. Kelly, who defended Vogles, Russell said Vogles had been in his employ at different times for 11 years past. Recently, at Liver- pool, he (Russell) was defendant in a slander case, in whichVogles was a witness against him,but it was not true that he tried to induce a man named Ronayne to assault Vogles. He did not try to arrange with a man named Mather at Warrington for anything of that kind. He had given coppers to Ronayne when he asked for money while drunk, but had never paid him any money as suggested by Mr. Kelly. A witness named Black, who was with Russell on War rington platform, confirmed his evidence as to Vogles conduct there, and the stationmaster's interference.— John Gordon, night stationmaster at Warrington, deposed to hearing the foul and provoking language used to Russell by Vogles. Witness locked Russell and Black in a first class carriage but unlocked the door as the train left. If a policeman had come up in time witness would have given Vogles into custody for his drunken and disorderly conduct. —For the defence Mr. Kelly represented that Russell struck Vogles in the subway at Earlestown and then ran away. Vogles followed in a state of excitement; he had had some drink at Warrington, but the police would not have apprehended him at Earlestown on the charge of being drunk, had not he been so excited by Russell's conduct. —Charles Lovesay, greengrocer, of Earlestown, said: I was with Vogles at Warrington, and came home with him. I got the tickets, and was not on the platform all the time. Then I lost Vogles, and I never saw him speaking to Russell. At Earlestown there wean bother but I could not say which of them caused it. I had had about five glasses of rum at Warrington.—Mr. Knowles: Did you tell the police you had about ten glasses of rum ?—Yes.--The Clerk then read over a second charge against Vogles, of being drunk and disorderly in Queen- street, Earlestown.—Vogles was sworn, and said he might have had four or five half-pints of beer that night at Warrington. It would take a good deal more than that to make him drunk. He never saw Russell on the kVarrington platform, but only in the train, and he never threatened or spoke to him. At Earlestyvvn he was walking up the subway with a man named Lovesay. Russell and Black came behind them. Black passed, but Russell punched him (Vogles) in the jaw and ran away very fast. Following more slowly, with the nten- tion of informing the police, he found Russell talking to the police, who apprehended him.- -Inspector Ellis said Vogles was mad drunk while in the cell at Earlestown. about 25 minutes after his arrest —After retiring for a few minutes the Chairman said the unanimous opinion of the Bench was that it was an- exceedingly serious case in which Vogles might be prosecuted for perjury. They bound him over to keep the peace for six months—himself in £lO and two sureties in £5 each. For the assault on Black, which might have been unintentional, they only required him to pay the costs.—The cross-summons against Russell was dismissed. and Vogles was fined 2s. Gd. and costs for drunkenness and disorderly conduct.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WIDOW'S TROUBLE. Mr. Brighouse opened an inquest at St. Helens, on Monday, on the newly-born child of Eliza Benson, a widow,of 178,Park-road. It was stated that the woman had been a widow with eight children for three years. S'ae whs in receipt of parish relief. She alleges that in consequence of being criminally assaulted by an unknown man she had a child born on Friday morning. She was alone at the time, and said that it was still- born. The medical officer of health, Dr. Buchan, inter- vened, and on making a post-mortem he reported that the child appeared to have had a separate existence The case was adjourned for the attendance of Mrs. Benson.</p>
        <p>A DIFFICULT EJECTION. Edward smith was charged at St. Helens on Tuesday with being drunk on licensed premises. Inspector Jackson said he found the prisoner very drunk in the Oddfellows' Arms, Parr Stocks-road. The landlady said he had just come into the house, and had had no drink. It took four men to get him out of the house. When brought to the police station he refused to give any name or address. He was sent to prison for twenty- eight days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>The Election of Guardians Sir,—As the time for the election of Guarditvas is at hand may las an advocate of temperance sugeest that it is undesirable for persons concerned in the drink trade, the great cause of pauperism, to be re-elected Guardians of the poor. Messrs. Davies. Payton and W. C. Brooks' ought to be opposed by those who have the real intergst of the people at heart. With what decency can they decide upon the cases of persons whose poverty may be traced to intemperance and how can they deal impartially with the assessment of licensed premises, a very important part of Poor Law work? Another class of persons who ought not to be on Boards of Guardians are the agents of insurance companies. There is a great temptation for such agents to get a name for being kind and generous and so to get more business for themselves and for their companies at the ratepayers' expense, by advocating the giving of relief in money to any who claim it. The Warrington Guardians, I believe, deal with relief in Committee and so the electors have little chance of learning how their work is done, but for the general reasons I have given it, will be well if Messrs. Ashcroft and Bolton are replaced by some substantial Itradesmen or property owners.—Yours respectfully, W. DAWKINS, Lovely Lane, February 21st, 1907. Have You Any Plants? Sir,—As your readers are aware,there are many people blessed with gardens who when the overhaulinr, season is at hand find themselves possessed of a surplus stock of plants for which they have no use. In the ordinary ' course the bulk of this is treated as rubbish and des- troyed. The Beautiful Warrington Society knotAing how the love of cultivating plants and flowers has gained a hold,desi re to help on and develop this splendid taste as far as possible. It is with the greatest delight that we view the progress which the taste for this pure and simple pleasure is making among the working classes of the community. The Society through the eenerosity of Messrs. CrOsfleld has secured a depot at Factory-lane, Warrington which is open for contribu- tions of plants from 9 to 12 and from 1 tO4 on each day of the week except Saturday when it will be open to receive in the morning only. All who delight in seeing the spread of this love of plants and flowers have now a splendid opportunity of assisting and when possible are invited to send their surplus stock to the Time Office, Bank Quay. We will on receipt of a post card make arrangements for the carriage of plants front those who 'have no facilities for sending them. The plants will in due course be distributed to persons who will undertake to try to grow them. It is hoped that this venture may be the means of furthering the cause of making. our town more beautiful. I am, Sir, yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>JAMES RYAN,</p>
        <p>Old Academy, Warrington, February 18th, 1907.</p>
      </div>
