      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Warrington's First Home Defeat.</head>
        <p>Warrington's First Home Defeat.</p>
        <p>Caturdav was a splendid nw fnr football, and 'a crowd of finite 6.000 thrilled up at Wildersnool to witness tho important match with Oldham. The visitors anneared at full strenrth,htit. Warrinzton were again handicapped by the absence of Fish. isherrrand, and Preaton. Still tho side was thought rood enough to win and local disappointment was keen when Oldham spoiled War- rington's hitherto unblemished record for home matches. Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>Warrington—Berry, back: Brooks, Dickinson, Taylor, and nockenhell. three-quarter backs ; Lees and Jenkins. half hacks; Heath. Ffarmer, Thomas, Boardman,Shugars and Naylor. forwards. . Oldham—Thomas, hack: Tyson. W. Dixon. B. Dixon. and Cash. three-quarter backs ; Lees and Yewlett, half back. Vergnann. Avery, Smith, Longworth, Vowles, and Tonham. forwerds. Referee. Ai.. IT. Erbrondson, Bradford. Touch indges, Messrs. J. Brittain. Leigh. and .1. nraham, St. Helens. Oldham won the toss. and started the game with a strong wind at their backs. They were quickly on the 'Neeres.ivo. and Berry had hhrd work to keep them at h‘r. Smart. forward play by Heath and Harmer put Warrington on the attack, but a lnng punt by Thomas sent them bark again. Ferguson twice trkd to lower thehorne goal from fairly (WY position.. but Ninat of the nlay remained in the home half. and Lees being at fault.Yewlett got away and sent the ball on to Tvarm, who heat Hnokenholl for a try at the corner. Fergnion failed at enal. Warrington made a fine rally '?ter thim. and Dickenenn received a wide Pass from .Tenkins, hut hesitation proved fatal. Pressure was maintained, and Hockenhall got away on the left. When he eonld have crossed it the corner he doubled inside and made a hrill ;ant rim for the posts, only to be hauled down under the her. This was Warrincton'a last chence this half for Oldham played a strnur game. Cash cleverly iinded Brooks and punted across the goal-month where l'nnham *nomad and scored an easy try when aeperently ofYsinle. Forenson added a goal. Just before he interval Lees had a kick charged neer the line, and rnol scrambled over at the corner. Half-time : Old- ham. 1 anal 2 trio,. (11 niintcl WArrinetnn, nil. On resoming Oldham again opened strongly. the forwards dribbling speedily to the home quarter. Some clever punting followed. end Brooks being slow in 'clearing was robbed by Thomas and Yewlitt. the former acnring an unexpected try near the posts which was not "imnroyeri 'non. This wee serious blow to the home side. but they set to work hard to reduce the deficit. The Combination was all at, sea, but by rushing the came Oldham were penned to the line, and twice en thing hilt mire lock Hayed them. Once a flying kick cleared to centre, and agnin an Oldham player fell down right in front of two forwards who were taking the hall nwer the line. Oldham were quite nyernlayed, hut defen.,ed strongly. and it was threequarter time before T-Tookenhall beat them with a cross-kick from which Brooks fielded end scored behind the posts. Thomas kicked a gnat Warrington had other chances in the later stages but failed to show their usual 'smartness. and Oldham won by 1- goal, 4 tries (14 points) to Warrington. 1 goal. 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Warrington v. Liverpool City.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Liverpool City.</p>
        <p>The nostponod rnettrh between Warrington and Liver- 'non' City was played on tho Warrington ground on Monday before 1,000 spectators. Heavy rain spoiled the play in the second half. Both sides were fairly well renresented. Teams :</p>
        <p>Warrington : Foster, full back; Fish, Cartwright, Dickenson and Brooks, three-quarters; Jenkins and Hockenhnll, half hiwks Prevfcn. Boardman, Naylor, Heath, Harmer, and Shugars, forwards. Liverpool City: Edmnnds, full back : Meadowcroft, Taylor, Lewis. end R.tcliffe, three-quarters ; Griffith' -and Mewl, full back ; Farmer, Ellis, Greenland, Thomas, Ashworth end Langley, forwards. S..inr that the City are quite out of their class in the Northern League, the game was not taken seriously by Virarrincton who went in for passing and running almost, exclusively. In the flrst few minutes a closer movement, between Brooks and Dickenson quite outwitted the defence and the last-named moored. After tries came regularly every two or three minutes. Preston scored two, Fish two, Hockenhall, Jenkins, Cartwright. Brooks and Bos,rdman one each. The goal- kicking was poor in the extreme. Hockenhall and Preston only getting one each. Half-time: Warrington 2 goals, 10 tries, 34 points. Liverpool nil. Rain descended very soon after the resumption and play became very scrappy. The homesters combined badly, but managed to get half-a-dozen more tries through Hockenhall two, Fish. Boardman, Forster and Cart- wright. Not, a single goal was kicked. Final : Warrington 2 goals, 16 tries, 52 points, to Liverpool nil. Warrington v. Widnes.</p>
        <p>Warrington fulfilled their postponed League match at Widnes on Thursday in fine, windy weather. There was a gate of about 2,000, half of whom came from Warring- ton. Teams : P _ _</p>
        <p>Warrington: Berry. full back; Hocke.nhall, Isher- wood, Dickenson and Brooks, three-quartera ; Lees and Jenkins, half backs ; Preston, Boardman, Thomas, Shngars, Heath and Naylor, forwards. Widnes : James, full hack ; Davies, Lambert, Warthy. end Taylor. three-quarters; Kennedy and Lally, halfbacks; S. Aspey, Argent, Turner, Carr, Rigby and Evans, forwards. Widnes won the toss but decided to face the wind, and Preston started the game. Jenkins and Dickinson at once got away smartly, hut the latter sent a bad pass to Brookes. Keeping up the attack, Warrington penned Widnes on the line, and Naylor scrambled over, Preston kicking a goal, Widnes made a brief incursion into Warrington territory, but 'I homas drove them hack and made an opening for Brooks, which was not utilised. A grand dribble by the home forwards took the ball to the line, where Brooks kicked dead. Dickenson took play to the home quarters again and during some vigor- ous play near the line, Boardman got hold and forced his way over, Preston again adding thegoal. warring. ton continued to show superior form and Isherwood gave Hockenhall a chance, but the wing man was not</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA'S original foe. Is still repelling INFLUENZA</p>
        <p>, , , . •• • . , ,• , • • 6 :; • . • •</p>
      </div>
