      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>MR.</head>
        <p>MR.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESrRVED.I THE CLERK AT PAY CORNER.</p>
        <p>BY BEATRICE lIERON-MAXWELL, Author of" Little Miss Nobody, " "The Adventures of a Lady Pearl Broker," "A Woman's Soul," "A Forbidden Lore,' "What May Happez,"</p>
        <p>"What name di a you say?"</p>
        <p>The clerk in the little 'wooden box that served as counting-house to New End Factory, stooped down to the pigeon-hole through which he had been passing wages to the "hands " and looked inquiringly for a face to match the voice that had just spoken to him. A pair of beautiful, weary eyes met his, and brightened with a faint touch of interest as their owner realised that instead of the usual elderly, by-as-dust paymaster a young man was presiding ,ver the dispensement of the weekly salaries. "Revere Halliday," she repeated more clearly, while a tinge of pink invaded pale cheeks, and an instant later a sovereign and some shillings were on the circular sill, and she had taken them and passed on. The yard was emptying rapidly now, for the employees in the accountants' office, of which she was one, were paid last, after all the workmen had taken their money and dispersed, so that only another girl or two, shorthand-typists like herself, and about thirty men-clerks remained still on the hither-side of the main gate; and as Revere walked towards it one of these hurried after her, and lifting his cap remarked: "New chap in the box. How's that?" nodding towards the little shed wedged into an angle of the wall that was sponsor for the name of "Pay Corner." Revere's face clouded.</p>
        <p>"I really don't know," she said, and quickened her steps. Evidently this dark-browed, thin-lipped man both annoyed and alarmed her. But Jonas Marne was not easily rebuffed. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Looks above his work," he Continued, keeping pace with her. "A gentleman by the cut of his clothes and the look of him. I wonder what the governors have put him in for." "It is really of no interest to me," said Revere. "I cannot see how it matters to any of us who pays, so long as we are paid. You must excuse ale, Mr. Marne. I want to get home early, and is is my way." She turned abruptly down a side street and left Marne staring in discomfiture after her. _ _</p>
        <p>He was actlie head of his batch of foremets, and but for a black cross standing against hie name from time to time in the conduct sheet he would have been by now in one of the most responsible positions of the factory. But his violent and overbearing nature led to many quarrels, and if there was a row in the London-road Marne was sure to be in it and to take more than his share of the fighting. Po that though his employers and his mates in the factory alike feared him, as men of strong natures are feared, no one made friends with him or had a good word to say behind his back. And since the day when an unguarded loiik and speech from him had shown Revere that she was the object of his admiration and possibly of some deeper feeling, she had shunned and hated him, and would walk a mile out of her way to reach New End rather than risk his companionship. She did not slacken her steps until she was sure that he had not followed her, and then she stopped to rest by a bit of fenced-in waste ground, beyond which was the street of shabby houses where she lodged.</p>
        <p>Tier thoughts followed her .eyea to the smoky haze of real London; for here, in this by-way, it seemed to be neither town nor suburb, and she murmured :</p>
        <p>"I shall have to go. Every day he makes some excuse to waylay me. I ant frightened of him, and I don't know why." She was ctill pondering over the extraordinary influence Jonas Marne had begun to exercise over her, when with a start she became aware that the footsteps of someone crossing the road had stopped close to her. Without looking round she turned to hurry on, when a pleasant voice said: "I beg your pardon, am I right for Factory- road ? " The relief she felt flashed into her face as she answered: "Yes ; it is the street facing yeu." The clerk lifted his hat, and recognising the voice and face that had attracted him before wondered what her expression meant. "I am afraid I startled you," he said, apologeti- mlly, "but. I am new here and have not learnt illy way about yet. All the streets sem exactly like each other. It's a monotonous place." "It is a monotonous life," Revere answered ; "six days of factory and one of rest, week in and week out."</p>
        <p>She recognised him also, and wondered, as ho had about her, at the incongruity of his appearanat with his position.</p>
        <p>Unconsciously, they were already criticising each other closely. Meeting him in the street by chance one woulii have said that he was a man who, if he did any work at all, worked for the sake of linvinc a pro- fession and not to earn his living. The intell..ctual, well-bred face, and the tall, well-clothed figure suggested the life of what the factory hands called a "swell." Yet he was the new pay-clerk in a blacking factory on the outskirts of ciVili a in! You must be very tired of it," he s::id taking in the listlessness of lier attitude and tie droop at the corner of her mouth.</p>
        <p>They had both stopped, and she was again leaning over the fence and looking towards St. Paul's, the dome of which glowed in the sunset. "But," he went on, "you could PI oily find some. thing better, no doubt, Miss Halliday, Peshapa you have outside interests that keep you here?" She smiled a little.</p>
        <p>"I might go further and fare worse. They pay one well and punctually at the factory. No, I have no interests of any kind. I I ova been alone in the world for about a year— e or ..n le my si.ier died. We came here because it was the drat work that turned up for me. I got it through ar advertisement as soon as I had finished sly shork hand course."</p>
        <p>"And you live by yourself?" "In Factory-road," she answered. "Th is the best place about here for quiet lodging.." "So I was told. My number is twea,-six." "Yea? I fancy there is a nice lancllady there. I hope you will be comfortable." It had suddenly occurred to her that though hie official position demanded a civil answer to a ci•i question, it did not. necessitate the confidence slut was involuntarily bestowing on 1 im. He lifted his hat again. "Thanks. night." And walked on. She was looking after him wl en l e turned and came back.</p>
        <p>Good-</p>
        <p>"May laak you something?" he raid. "I am a stranger in the land and want to le sure of my bearings. Is it usual for complaints about the wages to be made through the pay-clerk?" "It is against the rules. All complaints must be addressed by letter to the heads of the firm." "I thought so. Do you know anything of a foreman called Jonas Marne ? "</p>
        <p>The colour that flooded her face faded so quickly and left her ;to strangely pale that he apologised again.</p>
        <p>-"I beg you to forgive me," he said. "I. am :timid I have touched on a subject that is unpleasant to you." "It is not that," she said, hurriedly. "Mr. Marne is nothing to me—not e,en a friend. Until lately I had nrer spoken to him In leed, I have no wish to speak to him now, for lie—he frightens me. Ile is a dangerous man, lam told, and I I have an instinctive dislike for him."</p>
        <p>She was quite unaware of the ppeal that her stammering agitation made to him, Or that he was registering a mental resolve to frustrate any designs of Mr. Jonas Marne towards he...</p>
        <p>"I was not. prepossessed in his favour," he said, quietly, "and I shall report him for breaking the rules.'</p>
        <p>She looked more troubled. "I would not do that," she urged. "He boasts that he has never owed any man a grudge without paying it in full. Be careful of him, please. He will know that you reported him when he 'gets a r, trimand." "He will know more than t I..resently. Miss :Halliday, since chance has i 4. .I.sred us and we are-fellow-4orkers, may I venturo to count myself as an acquaintance of yours—a friend, I nhops, later. May I tell you my name?" "I should like to know .it," she answered, ,:oply. •</p>
        <p>• "I am Godfrey Drennan. May I call and sce imu to-morrow ?</p>
        <p>She hesitated. "My little sitting-room is very humble," she said, "and my 'Audi:v.ly is very severe. I think it would be better not:"</p>
        <p>"I am very lonely here," he pl, 're not even go for a walk together ? " She *hook her head, laughing. Mod of you.</p>
        <p>"Might</p>
        <p>"it is yarj</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 23, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>some peopte were oommg down the street towards them. Revere looked at her watch. "But I must be going home now," she con- cluded, "or Mrs. Dale will believe I am out to tea. Good-bye, Mr. Brennan." "Good-bye."</p>
        <p>They slio-ok hands this time, and she crossed the road. so that they walked apart until he reached No. 26, and went in. And Jonas Marne, loitering in the street a little further on, waiting for an opportunity of speaking to Revere again. looked suspiciously at both, and, muttering something to himself, turned on his heel and went away.</p>
        <p>_ ........ _ Had he known that Godfrey Brennan was watchinz him through the venetian blinds, the antilonthy that he already felt for the new clerk would have deepened. As it was, although Marne failed in his attempts to see Revere for a whole week, he did not definitely connect this fact with Brennan until one evening after work hours he met them walking together by the little canal that belonged to the factory. The sudden flash of keen and jealous suspicion, followed by an evil look of rage at Godfrey, told Revere that this chance encounter would mean mischief if Marne's reputation for paying off scores was a reliable one.</p>
        <p>She had drifted so easily and gradually into friendship with Godfrey, and it had begun to seem so natural that they should meet at the end of each day's work, and saunter homewards together, that she had almost forgotten her apprehension about Marne until this meeting. "Mr. Brennan." she said, quickly, when Jonas was out of hearing, "have you had any more difilcultv with Mr. Marne? Did you report him? "1 did. He will learn the result next pay-day." "He is not going to be dismissed ? " She clasped her hands in alarm.</p>
        <p>"I thought," answered Brennan, gravely, "that h. was not a friend of yours—that you disliked him, in fact. Why should his dismissal distress you ? " "Because he will revenge himself on you: he will believe it to be your doing. Did you see how he looked at you just now ? " "He is an insolent fellow. Don't let us waste our time talking about him." She laid her hand persuasively on his arm. "Don't make light of it," she said, earnestly; "from the moment that he is discharged you will be in danger. Will you promise to be careful, to give him no chance of attacking you?" Godfrey took the pleading hand in his. it matter to you?" he asked. She drew away from him, and was silent until he repeated his question.</p>
        <p>"Does</p>
        <p>"I should be lorry," she said, uif any friend of</p>
        <p>She broke off in surprise, for they believed them- selves alone on the canal path, and, at this moment, a man whom neither of them had per- ceived brushed past them so clumsily that he jerked the stick out of Godfrey's hand. "Sorry!" he said, briefly, picking up the stick nnd handing it to Godfrey. With a lift of his hat, he walked on, crossed one of the small bridges, and disappeared amongst the buildings on the other side.</p>
        <p>"Where did he wring from?" said Godfrey. "Rather queer his coming upon us like that. There was something familiar about him to me, too. Where CAA I have seen him before?" Revere looked troubled. "He reminded me of someone, but I do not really know him. He must have been in the shed we have just passed."</p>
        <p>The incident, slight as it was, had broken the thread of their conversation, and they did not revert to it.</p>
        <p>A vague disquiet had taken possession of Godfrey, and his mind was occupied with a trivial t,uety to which he could not find the answer. Where had he seen the stranger of the canal walk before? Again and again, during the twenty-four hours that followed, he found himself pondering on this lapse of memory, unimportant as it seemed; and the question of Jonas Marne's dismissal did not once occur to him until the moment for it arrived. It was a custom—of which no one knew the origin—that when a factory man was discharged, he separated from the rest, and, instead of going out by the main exit, passed through a turnstile at the side, called in irony "Amen Gate." There was a suppressed rrurintir amongst the crowd in the yard when Jonas Marne, with his week's wages and discharge-paper in his hand, broke away from the stream and made for tii turnstile, and looks of inquiry and dismay were exchanged by many of the men. "He'll raise Cain for that," remarked one of them, and a significant silence followed. Never- theless, there was no result either that night or during the next week, and people began to believe that Marne had resigned his post willingly and left the neighbourhood to better himself. But Revere, with a sinking at her heart that no reasoning could quell, waited, hour after hour, for something that she felt was coming: and deliberately avoided Godfrey, lest Marne should meet them together. But for this, she would have learnt two facts that remained unknown to her till afterwards: first, that Godfrey was being "shadowed," and next, that this shadow was not Jonas Marne.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, at noon, after the surveyor had gone through the factory, an order was suddenly circulated that everyone should assemble at two, instead of at four, at Pay Corner, where their wages would be given to thorn and freedom, to leave off work. _ _</p>
        <p>---A.-gra—t-uitous holiday was such a rare event that it was received withamazement ; and there were many surmises as to its possible mum Revere, wondering and still troubled, decided to ask Mr. Brennan the reason for it when she took her salary; and, as she stood at the little pigeon- hole, she said, softly: "What does it mean, Mr. Brennam ? "</p>
        <p>There was no answer, except that the money wa. impatiently pushed a little nearer to her, and, al she took it, she saw dimly through the ground- glass window that the pay-clerk had stepped back, with the evident intention of making no reply. Bewildered and hurt, her first impulse was to burry homewards, but the sense of impending danger to him was so insistent that she put away her pride, and, returning to the yard, skirted round to a path at the back of the shed, down which he must pass on his way to the bend accountant's .office, which it was his duty to close before leaving. The clang of the main gate had already assured her that the factory was empty; and she waited, patiently at first, end later with growing alarm, for Godfrey to come.' At last she could bear it no longer. It was against the rules to be in the factory-yard after working hours, but she decided to risk being seen by the caretaker, and, running quickly across to the pay-shed, she tapped at the door. There was no sound in reply, and she turned the handle.</p>
        <p>The door yielded to her touch and opened. Across the floor, lying on his face, where he mist have fallen when someone from behind struck at him, lay this pay-clerk, his head battered beyond all recognition. In his outstretched hands he still clutched twc canvas bags, and from the open month of one some sovereigns had rolled and scattered themselves around him.</p>
        <p>-.......- ....... ; A wave of overwhelming horror swept over Revere, and, with a paaeionate cry ol his name, she recoiled and sank fainting on the floor.</p>
        <p>A moment later, one who was running in hot baste and heard the desperate call "Godfrey! Godfrey!" readied the shed, and, lifting her, carried her outside and held her in his anus while her senses were slowly coming back to her. As memory revived with them, she shuddered and closed her eyes, the deadly faintness sweeping over her again. "gem.," whianertd a tender voice. white lips</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>orusnea 'ner eneelc --you called me and 1 am here. Be brave, darling, for my sake. I must go and see to that poor fellow." And, openiag her eyes. she understood that a miracle had happened, for it was Godfrey who held her to his heart.</p>
        <p>The extraordinary circumstances of the New End Factory murder were a nine-days' wonder. It appeared that the large sum paid out in weeky wages had attracted the attention of a noted area-crackstuan, known as "Toff Jim " ; and his plans for obtaining it were so cleverly made that they would have succeeded but for one small item he had not taken into account—the dismissal of Jonas Marne.</p>
        <p>The fact that the new pay-clerk, Godfrey Brennan, bore a strong resemblance to "Toff Jim seemed, to that gentleman, a personal favour on the part of Providence that should not be wasted; and, having studied his "double" until every detail of clothing and general appearance was correctly reproduced, he had only two difficulties to over- come—obtaining possession of the bags of money, and getting the pay-clerk out of the way. Skeleton keys, of ingenious make, smoothed the first obstacle, and a telegram, purporting to Come from the head of the firm. to Brennan. impera• tively summoning him to the City at two o'clock, removed the other.</p>
        <p>Another telegram to the surveyor arranged tilt substitution of an earlier hour for paying the wages, and "Toff Jim," looking, to a casual observer, exactly like Godfrey Brennan, reached Pay Corner unhindered, with his booty and the pay-sheet, on which each name and the sum due tc it, appeared.</p>
        <p>The base coin which he handed through the pigeon-hole had been so carefully prepared that it roused no suspicion, and probably the only hint of possible discovery was Revere's ,question : "What does it all mean, Mr. Brennan ? '</p>
        <p>The instant that the main gate closed, "Toff Jim" was prepared to make his escape to the canal-path, where a confederate awaited him, and it was as he stooped to pick up the two bags that Jonas Marne, hidden all the time at the back of the shed, opened the door and dealt the first fatal blow, followed up by others that were intended for disflgurement.._</p>
        <p>"I' meant to do for him, and spoil his looks too," be said, savagely, when he was caught and con- fessed himself guilty. "Who was he, to come and steal the only girl I ever wanted ? I swore that if I couldn't have her, he shouldn't. He put a spoke in his own wheel, as well as mine, when he got me dismissed." But the mitigation of his sentence to penal servitude for life, because he had murdered the wrong man, doubled his punishment in reality; and his insensate rage at the blunder he had made and the thought that Godfrey and Revere would be happy during the long years while he was working out his sentence proved too much for him, and killed him in the first week of it.</p>
        <p>It was, strangely enough, at the very hour of his deat h that they signed and sealed their bond of happiness. "I have a confession to make to yon," Brennan said, as they walked home together in the dusk. "To-morrow is my last day as pay tlerk."</p>
        <p>"You are going to leave New End ? " she asked, in dismay. "No. lam going up a step—that is all. There is a tradition in the factory that every member of the firm must have served his apprenticeship by doing subordinate work in it before he can be partner. His qualifications are money to invest, and business capacity, which must be demonstrated in a practical way. So I agreed to take the post of pay-clerk for a while, and to live the life of a man who holds it in the ordinary way. My time is up to-morrow, when I serve at Fay Corner for the last time; and one of my concluding duties will be to ask Miss Revere Halliday for her resignation. The firm no longer requires leer services."</p>
        <p>She looked at him, startled and tremulouz, but his eyes reassured her. "We have another post to offer her," he said, putting his arms round her. "We want her to be the junior partner's wife." [Tux Exn.l</p>
      </div>
