      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>A MODELLING DUCIIESS.</p>
        <p>The Duches6 of Rutland, who has a studio in BelgraTia, has been taking lessons in modelling, ligure-drawing, and paAel-painting. Her daugh- ter, Lady Victoria Manners. and the countess of Wemyss have joined the Duchess in these studies. The Duchess of Rutland's artistic talent is remarkable, and her wonderfully-executed drawings of well-known people7most of which bay° also 0-1? Of IY?ing singularly 'faithful hikenst-- are prcirtal and ireasured in half the great houses in England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NAVY SETIGE COSTUME.</head>
        <p>NAVY SETIGE COSTUME.</p>
        <p>For early spring wear a costume of navy serge is always useful. Our sketch shews one realised in a rather bright shade of navy blue, trimmed_ with a loosely-woven silk braid. The smart bolero-like coatee is drawn in at the waist with a belt of tartan-patterned patent leather. and !inislied with a short fluted basque. The strap of braid over the shoulders is continued down beneath the belt to the edge of the basque. and !here is. also a strap over each shoulder. finishing ui a point over the sleeves. The revers are facea with a light green and blue plaid silk, and the fronts are adorned with some blue enamelled</p>
        <p>buttons and fine cord loops. The full sleeves finish at the elbow with a cuff trimmed with braid and a pretty kilted frill of tartan silk. The skirt is arranged with two stitched-down box- pleats in front, and trimmed with two rows of braid.</p>
