      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>MOSEY IN EMBROIDERY.</head>
        <p>MOSEY IN EMBROIDERY.</p>
        <p>As almost every girl nowadays has some regu- lar occupation or work, I can suggest an outlet for her energy and abilities, says a writer in the Tatier. Any girl with a little taste and business organisation ought to be able to make a quan- tity of money just now put of embroidery. Every second dress—if not more—in Paris is thickly hand-worked just now, and it is a fashion rapidly gaining ground over here and one im- possible to copy by machinery, so that hand work will become highly paid. A capital little busi- ness could easily, be worked up by any enterpris- ing girl by embroidering frocks and cloaks for either private customers or undertaking this part of the work for the big dressmakers and shops. Tho demand for embroideries on frocks is far in excess of what can be undertaken by the dress- makers' regular hands, who besides the time often do not possess the necessary experience for the work demanded by the fashions of the moment. Girls who have been wasting their talents on useless fancy work ought now to be able to make quite a nice little sum of money by the embroidering of skirts and trains for evening and court dresses. THE GIRLS MEN LIKE.</p>
        <p>Man is a queer creature, and however much he may say to the contrary, and however much he may encourage the jeune flue" to urge on her independent career, yet unquestionably, de- clares " Ambrosia " in the World, he has a sneaking admiration for the girl whose mother has been very "particular" with her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EVENING CORSAGE.</head>
        <p>EVENING CORSAGE.</p>
        <p>Our illustration depicts a dainty evening corsage of snow-whit l voile, trimmed with cross- over frills, medallions, and seed pearls, and will be found a most becoming style for a slender figure. The sleeves are of the one-puff descrip- tion, finished with hills of lace at. the elbow;</p>
        <p>and i will be noticed that they are slightly longer than those worn a season or two ago. They are also a criange from the "no-sleeve" or strap which has enjoyed such a long vogue. The waist is finished with the narrower waist- band now so high in women's favour, and may be white, of the voile, or fashioned in a contrast- ing shade of velvet.</p>
