      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>OPERA CLOAKS.</head>
        <p>OPERA CLOAKS.</p>
        <p>Nothing can be more defiant than the even- ing cloaks of to-day, says the Queen. So many of them are made in transparent fabrics trimmed with most costly lace and furs. Some of them have large sleeves, and are more of the mantle shape, while others favour the burnouse style, made in soft satin, with lace let in. The softer. the more clinging, .the more fashionable they are. There is a great deal of originality. Happily, almost every material is used—silk, satin, velvet, and cloth, chiffon, and lace, linecl with some soft colouring. The half-fitting, loose cape coat, with its hanging sleeves is comfort- able at this time of year: for this sleeves, are using the most beautiful materials, such as rieh bso- eades. Loose, wide wrap cloaks are sometimes lined throughout with fur. These are of the circular shape, so that one end can be thrown over the shoulders. This is very cqmfortable and very elegant; but it requires a great deal of making, and can only be turned out well by first-ratq hands Fur is used inside and outside the evening cloaks</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>SCALLOPED OYSTERS.—Open them in the hollow shell. and stew them in their own liquor for one minute, then remove them with a spoon nd beard; pass the liquor through a sieve: add- ing a lump of butter in proportion with the number of oysters. When this butter is melted throw in as many breadcrurnl-43 as the butter will absorb. add the oyster liquor, and give all me boil. Have ready as many well-buttered shells as ,von nsy require, strew •them w• ith fine breadcfminbs lightly spiced with white P,PPer and nutmeg to taste. then put in a layer r.f oysters. newt some breAcrundls, then a layer ••F oysters: moisten with the liquor and. strew the whole with fine breadcrumbs, sr..attering 7.ttle of butter over these here and there; add tc deFsrtspoonful of cream, and set be- • the fire,- or in an oven, until ths, lops are of a light golden colour, or when theroughli heated ,hold over them a hot salamander. Sen; to table lurtli halt IeRAOIIS and cayenne.—Queen</p>
      </div>
