      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>VOTES FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>VOTES FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Liberals' Views.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Liberals on Tuesday evening dis- cussed the question of women sufferage and by a majority of two to one endorsed the principle of the extension of the franchise to the weaker sex. There was a fair attendance at the meeting, which was held in the Providence schoolroom, Mr. W. H. Sherbnrit presiding. Mr. E. Lloyd Jones introduced the subject by moving "That this meeting calls upon the Government to pass a measure dealing with women suffrage during the present year." Never in the history of England, he said, were women so determined as they were at present in their desire to assist in making the laws which they were called upon to obey,and in their demand for political justice. Women's rights and priveleges should not be confined to the tout walls of their own homes. They demanded a vote because it was legislation which dictated the conditions of labour and which provided for the educa- tion of their children. Were they not concerned in all legislation? Men's interests were women's interests ; their interests were identical. Were women not inter- ested in every question that affected their husbands? It was a monstrous injustice that one half of the popula- tion should be without political rights, and it was an injustice that should be speedily removed. The cry of enfranchisement was not a cry of envious and discon- tented women, but was the expression of a desire to be of service to mankind, to build up a State founded on the eternal principles of love and justice, which could only be established through the unselfish co-operation of human activity. Discussion was then invited but no one, for a t in)* rose to speak, and eventually the Chairman seconded the motion. He did not see :anything these" out- rageous " women had done within the last few days which deserved such severe condemnation. It was certainly a serious question so far as women were con- cerned. The movement was not a recent one, but had been going on quietly for a great number of years. Mr. Plinsell had to be " outrageous " before he could get the reform carried with which his name was identified, and to say that the women who had been so prominent of late had been " outrageous " was only saying that what history was telling them was wrong. (Hear, hear.) Even yet the audience evinced no desire to enter into the debate nut when Mr. Loosey, so to speak broke, the ice by supporting the affirmative,there was no lack of speakers. One gentleman venturea npon a brief resume of history from the time of the Judaic laws up to modern periods. He adduced one interesting fact, that i recent excavations at Pompeii had resulted n the dis- covery of election addresses issued by women, which he said,showed that women held a high position in the political life of that time. Mr. Albinson moved the rejection of the resolution urging that the majority of women did not desire the franchise and that its granting would lead to political dissention in the household. A similar strain was taken by other opponents whilst one gentleman objected to the nation on the ground that women were not as well qualified as men to control state aff sirs. Ultimately the resolution, as stated above, was approved.</p>
        <p>The extraordinary spectacle of twenty-one elephants and sixteen camels suffering from a severe lapse from sobriety was witnessed recently at Peru, Indiana. They hclouged to a travelling menagerie just arrived from e tropics, and a mixture of whisky and bran had been prescribed to protect them from the cold.</p>
