      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TRE EXAIVITNER.</head>
        <p>TRE EXAIVITNER.</p>
        <p>FEBRUARY 23rd. 1907</p>
        <p>There no longer seems to TIE SECTS AND be any doubt that Mr. TELE RATES. McKwYNk will signalise his first year as President of the Board of Edimstion by an effort to remove the cost of denominational teaching from the public funds. It may he rememhered that this proposal was embodied in both Clause 111. and Clause IV. of the Education Bill of last year, and that it never received so much as a word of criticism from the opponents" of the Government in either House of Parliament. It is declared, 4Pnarently with authority, that a Bill is to bB. introduced for the purpose of removing the chief grievance of the passive rosisters. Local authorities, we are told, will pay the teachers' salaries in full, and they will then recover from the managers an amount proportionate to the cost of undenorninational teaching. This will by no means get rid of all the grievances imposed by the Act of 1902, for the management of the schools 'will still be in the hands of the denominations,and they will still have the right to appoint teachers subject to a religious test. But it will get rid of the anomaly that sectarian teaching should be paid for out of rates, and will probably have the effect of Putting an end to passive resistance. The friends of voluntary schools, too, will have some difficulty M opposing such a measure. When the Court of Appeal decided that the Act of 1902 really meant that there was no obligation on the local authority to pay for denominational teaching, there were many leaders of the Church who were prepared to accept the position. One of them, Lord CROSR has even gone so far, since the judgment was reYersed,and since the Education Bill was wrecked as to advise Churchmen voluntarily to pay for the denominational teaching in their schools. Nevertheless, we have no doubt that the measure Will he violently opposed. It was this very pro- ,l2'sel which induced Lord firrott CHM to threaten 13saaive resistance on the part of 'Churchmen; and the extremists, who, against the advica of their TtnrB moderate co-religionists, persuaded the 4e of Lords to wreck Mr. BIRR ELL'S Bill, will certainly raise a great deal of trouble. They argue that the provisions of this new Bill a're lien Worse than those proposed by Mr. BIRRELL. e did offer them rent for their echools.`Mit they (4.1.6 warned,lmng, before it was too late.that if they ;elected Mr. BrnnEnn's terms they were never likely °bcirepeated Besides Mr. BIRRELL offered rent in e°osideration of the schools being controlled and anaged by the representatives of the public who hrld the money. It is certain, too, that Mr. Molisisma will now insist on the provisions of the Act 0f.1902 being observed in their entirety; and for their present position the voluntaryists cannot blame anybody but themselves. They will have to find the money to put their schools in Rood repair, and Mr. McKENNA's Bill will impose °la them also the necessity of paying for the sectarian teaching given in their schools. Their SUCCOSS in meeting these obligations will be the Measure of the real demand for definite dogmatic instruction.</p>
        <p>We venture to think that before many years are over those who are responsible for the carry- ing on of Church schools will be crying out for the terms which the Liberal Government offered them last year. They then lost the opportunity of establishing denominationalism as part of the State system. They lost a million a year in hard cash and the free repair of all their school build- ings, which are used for other purposes as well as day school teaching. They lost what nearly every Parish in the country desires—i.e. educational Peace. But instead of taking the otter by which they could have lived at peace with all men, they deliberately provoked war. Fortunately the /louse of Commons still holds the purse-strings, 'lnd can dictate the terms under which public Illoney may be voted.</p>
        <p>Warrington Minister's Call to Stoke. The Rev. Alfred Barratt late of Ebenezer Church, Adutterma.rket-street, has received and accented a Imanimous invitation to Stoke-on-Trent and will begin his ministry there on Sunday April 7th 1907.</p>
