        <p>-- Write for fall particulars of above and rn.ny other lobs end You will learn why ganohester Property Investments are the safest la tbe enuntrv• MR. CLAYTON, le, Albert Square, Manchester.</p>
        <p>Warrington gave itself up GE.NER&amp;L BULLER'S to rejoicings on Thursday, VISIT, the occasion of Genaral Buller's visit to unveil the South Lancashire War Memorial which adorna the Queen's Gardens. The sun shone brightly, banners and streamers fluttered in the breeze, and a gay crowd thronged the streets. But we cannot truthfully say that everything passed off without a hitch. General Buller, one of our most popular military heroes, arrived safely, drove through the town escorted by the mounted infantry, and ' subsequently unveiled the dignified and lifelike bronze presentment of gallant Colonel O'Leary, who fell at Pieter's Hill when leading the South Lancashires. All these things were carried out according to schedule, more or less, but there was still something lacking. People were dis- appointed. What was it The procession! Somehow everyone had got the idea that there was to be an imposing military display and a grand triumphal march. The soldiers were there, the hands were there, and the General was there, but the procession was non est. After the unveiling ceremony the soldiers remained behind, and the General escorted by a handful of the local mounted infantry drove through the streets in a carriage en route to Stockton Heath. It was not an ininosing spectacle, and the public were frankly disappointed. If the Mayor and the Corporation, the soldiers and the bands had escorted General Buller through the town there would have been d pageant more fitting to the occasion and more gratifying to the assembled thousands. The whole affair was singularly unirn pressive considering its historic importance, and we cannot but think that with better management the occasion might have been made worthier the memorial and more in keeping with the dignity of the town.</p>
        <p>A cordial welcome has been AIR. CROSFIELD extended to the Borough AT WARRINGTON. Member this week •end by all classes of the community —except the Conservatives, who took the oppor- tunity of trying to give him a stab in the back. We might perpetrate a not inappropriatebull by saying that the stab" missed fire." The Liberals and workingmen generally were particularly pleased to see Mr. Crosfield looking so much better for the holiday in the South of France which he took at the close of last session. Mr. Crosfield is not a man of cast-iron constitution, and those of his constituents who expect him to practically live in the House while it site, and spend the vacations at Warrington, are rather too exarting in their con- ception of an M.P.'s duty. The Member for Warrington has taken. a deep interest in the progressive legislation which has been a feature of the work of the Government up to now, and his close acquaintance with the pro- visions of the most important measures is highly commendable. The fox-hunting squire type of " " who did not trouble his head about the nature of the Bills before him, but voted in the party lobby on all occasions and sundry, is a rara avis nowadays. There must be very few members of the present businesslike and democratic House of Commons who do not take a personal interest and responsibility in all that is done. Mr. Cros- field is one of these conscientious politicians, and in his review of the year's work which he gave to that magnificent meeting on Monday evening he showed clearly that "the game of politics "is to him a serious business. We hope nothing will occur to prevent him displaying fully that devo- tion to the Liberal cause with which we know he is imbued.</p>
      </div>
