      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>General 13u11er's mild, unassuming and affable behaviour captured all hearts. For such a warrior he was remarkably pacific.</p>
        <p>* * Most appropriately, Horrid Weather and Dreary Day were two of the dogs who won their courses at the Waterloo contest on Wednesday. , * * *</p>
        <p>The soldiers' memorial was nearly unveiled before its time, as owing to the high wind which prevailed on Sunday the Union Jack which concealed the statue from view was almost torn to ribbons.</p>
        <p>It was pretty generally known beforehand that Mr. Keir Hardie was held in btd odour at Cambridg3 but until he bumped up against a hollowed-out orang, skin filled with sulphurretted hydr:Ten he didn't know just how bad that odour was.—" London Opinion." , * *_*</p>
        <p>The Tory handbill dist-tibuted among the people who attended the Liberal meeting on gonday provided a lot of ill-mannered questions which nobody asked. Instead, Mr. Crosfield was greeted with the utmost cordiality. Political enthusiasm was also repeatedly manifested by the audience. o o</p>
        <p>There is nothing but warm praise for the splendid piece of work which Mr. Alfred Drury has accom- plished in the South Lancashire Memorial. People who knew the late Cilonel MacCarthy O'Leary say that the artist has caught his likeness most faithfully, and there is no doubt that the statue is a splendid example of the sculptor's art. It is most certainly an ornament to the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley waxed *eloquent in his "Budget speech" at the meeting of the Education Committee on Monday. One of the choicest passages was that in which he described the Technical School as being a building of which the ratepayers might be justly proud and "none the less so since it was not a product of posthumous benevolence nor one of those crumbs of conscience which at irregular intervals fell from multi-millionaires' tables, but had been pro- vided for themselves by themselves." Yes, ratepayers are painfully aware of the fact. * *</p>
        <p>Captain Waring who on Monday was returned for Banffshire in place of the late Mr. A. W. Black, is a cousin of Mr. Lamont,Parliamentary private secretary to Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman. He married in 1901 Lady Clementina Hay, daughter of the Ma;•quis of Tweedale. He fought well but unsuccessfully in Wigtownshire at the last General Election. Captain Waring s father, who was a member of the firm of Waring Bros., railway contractors, died in 1887, leaving half a million sterling. The new member for Banffshire is an ex-officer of the Ist Life Guards, and was the tallest officer in the regiment. Tic is descended on both sides from Yorkshire families. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Harvey, M.P., the new member for North East Derbyshire made an excellent impression on the big working class audience in Parr Hall on Monday evening. In appearance he is a big, burly man and looks as if he had spent 20 years in the mines, which is the case. For a man of little education, Mr. Harvey is an effective speaker, and the way he put the case of the mining royalties before the audience was most convincing. His facts were clear, and his figures positively illuminating to the hundreds of iron-workers present. Another illustration of his showed the differ- ence between Tory and Liberal legislation as regards the operations of the Workmen's Compensation Act. As it stands now, amended by the present Government that Act is an instrument capable of being turned to the full benefit of any injured worker, even the poor illegitimate child. e on The unveiling ceremony in Queen Gardens on Thursday afternoon may have been perfectly satisfac- tory to those people who got within the privileged circle, but the crowd outside saw little, and many of them were holders of nice pink tickets which said "admit by Bold-street entrance." Long before the time fixed for the ceremony, Bold-street and Palmyra Square were packed with a dense throng and the gate was quite inaccessible. Even if it had been it was not open, and a largei number of exasperated ticket- holders went away n disgust. The Chairman of the Committee apologises courteously, in another column for this" untoward circumstance." What puzzled all those without the gates was, why some of the soldiers or volunteers could not have been utilised to keep a passage through the streets to the entrance to the gardens. Apparently no attempt was made to main- tain a thoroughfare. The few police on duty were good-humoured but helpless. It was a first-class botch</p>
      </div>
