      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 23, 1907. LIBERAL MEETING AT THE PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 23, 1907. LIBERAL MEETING AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>to sixty-five years of age, so that we might get a pension then—(laughter)—asking us, in fact, to do things just as sensible as feeding a dog with its own tail. (Langhter.) However much they might differ from Mr. Chamberlain in politics in his later days. they must all respect the immense energy and courage he had always shown—(a voice: "Good old Joe ")—and not forget some of the good Radical work he did before he got entangled in that sad South African embroglio. (Applause.) He was not going to enter into that ques- tion, although the financial effects were still felt in the country. (Hear, hear.) Thee was, after all, a SILVER LINING</p>
