        <head>Speeches by Mr. W. E. Harvey, M.P. and Mr. W.</head>
        <p>Speeches by Mr. W. E. Harvey, M.P. and Mr. W.</p>
        <p>Clause which, while they were legislating for this great Protestant country, gave to minorities the rights to which they were thoroughly entitled and there- fore, he thought, provided a proper solution of this difficult question which so long as the country set its face against a secular solution, was surely as ably and well thought out as anything could possibly he. (Applause.) Dr. Macnarnara used some pretty scathing words of Mr. Balfour's action in that case, and he (Mr. Crosfield) was sure it was little short of a national scandal that all that time and labour should be thrown away and that things should be left in their present utterly unsatisfactory condition. (Apolause.) As to the alteration in the relation between Lords and Commons, it was certain to come. It was only a ques- tion of time. He was sure they might trust a good deal to the prudence of their most sagacious leader. (Applause.) Lord Lansdowne, a very admirable states- man, had been perfectly frank and he said that the quntion was to be settled in the way that would suit his own House best and that they would choose their ground and their time. He (Mr. Crosfield) did not think the Premier would quite take Lord Landowne at his word and there would have to be a little patience. They might have and he hoped they would have one or two good strong Liberal budgets passed first. (Applause.) There might be a little fencing and skirmishing before they came to close quarters on that; great Constitutional issue, which rather reminded him a what happened in the 18th century when there was a dontest in the House of Lords between the Earl of Chatham and another gentleman called Sir Richard Strachan. Four lines were written about the controversy : Lord Chatham, with his sword drawn, Stood waiting for Sir Richard Strachan ; Sir Richard, longing to be at 'em, Stood waiting for the Earl of Chatham. (Laughter.) Continuing, Mr. Crosfield said he had spoken of measures affecting this country,but he would like to say that of all the things that had happened in the past year the most impressive and not the least important was the great gathering of the representatives of twenty-one different nations, not with a view to putting an end to arma- ments—they had not got their heads in the clouds awl were not crying for the moon—but to help on the great cause of</p>
