        <p>to that dark cloud and that was what they had , always adruired,—the chivalrous and humane spirit which our soldier showed when once Iwe had to fight our battle and win it, in ! South Africa. Next Thursday would indeed ha a (memorable day, when they met to honour a man who was not only a plucky soldiers but one whom everyone respected as a gallant and true-hearted Englishman. as to whether Mr. Balfour would accept a settlement or (APPla.usi..) In conclusion he would only say to their not. He did not want to use extravagant language, but I friends on the other side—for he had many friends on he thought that nobody who witnessed the weeks and the other side and he did not know he had ever quarrelled months of toil that men of the very first capacity like personally with a Conservative because he was a Mr. Birrell—(applause)—and another splendid man on Conservative—that they had a pretty long innings, and education who had just joined the Cahi nat., Dr. Nlacnalaa ra , they must not expect the swing of the pendulum, as —(applause)—devoted to the mei-sari?, how every it was called, to be too rapid. He rather thought they detail was scrutinised, and who Saw the humoiir the would find the pendulum was going to swing a long patience,the good tetxtper,and the spirit of compromise time the other way. (Applause). He was afraid they in which Mr. Birrell piloted the Bill, could fail to feel were aptl to count a little bit on the gullibility of sick and sorry that so much time and effort should be democracies, and to draw comparisons with what thrown away because men in the House of Lords who, happenr?clin ancient history. There was, however, this for the most part, really knew very little about the great distinction between the present democracy and question, lent themselves to a mere party game of any that had gone before it in the world's history. The tactics at the bidding of Mr. Balfour. As to the last few generations had witnessed a marvellous develop- question of the House of Lords he (Mr. Crosfield) meat of science, and of the guidance it had given to thought they ought to deal with it with perfect good humanity, and they also had what was lacking in the past—education. (Applause). It was because they humour but also with perfect firmness. (Hear, hear). recognized the vital importance to the advancement Of He was certain that the representatives of constituences the nation that eduction meant, that they felt such dis- all through the country sitting on the Liberal side of gust that .the question should be made a matter of the House recognized that --it was now absolutely party tactics. (Hear, hear.) The fact that there was now impossible to go on as they were doing, that a mere education to guide the democracy meant that people tinkering with the complexion of the Lords would not be sufficient—(hear, hear)—that whatever individuals were reading and thinking for themselves in a way they might think-, the great majority of the people of this never did before. He believed they would see that the country were wedded to the system of a Second Be i Bench of Ministers, a score of men of the very first Chamber, but that the veto of that Second Chamber ability n the House, were steering a sane and sober should not be irrevocable and final. (Applause.) He course !orward, not wandering into the tyranny of worse was surprised that when concession after concession was militarism on the one hand or into, the still V made upon the Education Bill with a view to meeting tyranny of the hopeless and impossible gospel of the wishes of denominationalists in every possible way, Socialism on the other hand. (Applause). He hoped TIIE LORDS HAD BROUGHT ABOUT AN that if the recital he had given of the measures that had ISSUE been passed in the last year had been a little dry, they as quickly as they had done. He daresay they won ld,ns the phrase went, "ca-canny " for a bit and let other would admit that it was measures slip through. Education had been before the . PRACTICAL WORKI hica country as much even as the question of Free Trade and increase the happiness of the mass of people in this would do something to raise the status and n- bad been thoroughly debated with a view to establish- . : ing the great principle of popular control and abolish- country. (Applause.) That, after all, was why they ing what he himself considered odious—the system ' were in the House. Good government, progress and of tests—(applause)—which was bound to lead to improvement began at home and it was the condition of our own people that they had to think of first. But he, hypocrisy, shut out the man with the strength and , for one. often felt that while that was a great and noble courage of his convictions, and lot in the man who sub- ! b itself, it was not the only object they had to o j i .ect n ! scribed indifferently to anything. No two principles , ; strive. for in the Hous.e,. because in this sea-girt land we could have been more thoroughly thrashed out in the were in a happier position than most other countries, country. The Bill most admirably put them into force and he hoped that while they did their duty to our own and it did more, because it emphasised a principle , i people. it might be the proud privilege of this Progres- which had been dear to Liberalism throughout its his- , sive its to hold aloft the lamp which might tory, and which he for one would always stand by—the principle of toleration. (Applause). He had heard Mr. I shed its light into countries beyond the seas—the light Birrell in the House say that probably the country of liberty and of progress, encouraging less fortunate expected the Education Bill minus Clause 4, but he (NIT... people to follow in the direction in which we were Crosfield) valued the Bill all the more because of that ' travelling. (Loud applause).</p>
        <p>VOTE OF CONFIDENCE. Mr. E. J. GUEST proposed the following resolution: —" That this meeting desires to express its unabated confidence in Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. It thanks him for his labours in Parliament during the past sessions and hopes that he may long be spared to support our grand leader, Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman, in the passing of those democratic measures of reform,so much needed, for the general welfare of tne people." He thought Mr. Crosfield had served them remarkably well. • There had been a little undercurrent of grumbling, that Mr. Crosfield had not been amongst them as often as they had hoped, but it was started on the other side. Mr. Crosfield had been there oftener than their late member, and whenever he had failed to be present he had given a good reason. He had never been absent from any important division in the House of Commons unless through ill-health, and then he had always been careful to secure a pair with the Opposition. They trusted he would not only be spared long, but spared with vigour. (Applause.) Their Tory friends had been twitting them and saying that whilst they had had a great deal to say before the election about Chinese labour very little hail since been doae. He could assure their opponents that they still looked with horror upon Chinese labour but that when an evil had been com- mitted it was no easy matter to rectify it but that it would be rectified. (Applause). MR. HARVEY'S ADDRESS. In seconding the resolution, Mr. W. E. HARVEY, M.P. who had an enthusiastic reception, said he was a life long Liberal and had been connected with Liberalism from the day when he knew anything about the history of his country. (Applause). He had never ceased to declare against the impossible and impracticable doctrine of shaking hands with the moon and nursing the stars in their lap. He believed that the future of the country rested with intelligent Liberalism and Labour and that the great social problems of the age would be solved by that school of thought. (Applause). He had something to say about the House of Lords and was not going to mince matters for the will of the people could no longer be frustrata.cl by an irresponsible, unelected and unauthorised body known as the Lords. An intelligent nation would soon end that, and the House of Lords only existed to-day because of the ignorance of the people. An intelligent and intellectual people would refuse at once to allow a body of men born into certain positions, whether they , had capacity or not, to frustrate the will and design of ! the nation. (Applause). He had no objection to a Second Chamber, but he wanted that Second Chamber to be elected for intellectual capacity.- (Hear, bear). THE LAND QUESTION. He wanted them to appreciate the seriousness of the land question. Two-thirds of the land of England was owned by the members of the House of Lords, and 42 millions of people had to ask them if the land could be cultivated, and 800,000 men and collrery proprietors had to ask the Lords whether any coal or minerals should be taken out of the earth, and the ignorance of the people allowed it to go on. (Hear. hear.) He was ea confiscator—he did not believe in robbery—but he did believe in restoring stolen property to those to whom it belonged. (Laughter and applause.) That matter came very near to their daily lives. They had heard it great deal about foreign competition and Mr. Chamberlain had given them one solution. He would give them another. There was one great question they could rest assured he would not allow to remain behind—the royality laws of this country—applause)—which were an abomination. He had gone so thoroughly into this matter that he did not fear contradiction by any living man of his facts : Were the people aware that on every one of the millions ; of tons of coal produced a royalty of sixpence had to be paid to the lord of the manor ?—to the men sitting in the House of Lords, who would never have known there had been any coal there at all had it been left to them. (Applause.) Royalties were paid on every ton of iron smelted to the amount of 4s. Gd., while on steel it was G. In Germany, France and Belgium the royalties were, coal Bd., iron is. id. and steel in proportion, and there was also the great difference that in those countries the income from royalties went to the Chancellor of the Exchequer for the reduction of taxa- tion. If we took off this embargo, this inequity, this injustice, the working men of this country could hold their own against all corners. He (Air. Harvey) had not lost faith in our working men, who had been too unfairly handicapped. This question must come up for solution. The land once belonged to the people and they must have a Royal Commission—he would ask to be on it—to inquire how it had get into the hands of the landlords. (Applause.) LABOUR LEGISLATION. Mr. Harvey proceeded to refer to the labour legislation accomplished by the present Government. He had laid his claims in regard to workmen's compensation before the Tory ministers. He asked that compensation should begin from the day of the accident but his request was ignored. How had the present Government treated that request ? Under the old Act a man had to wait three weeks before he received compensation, but under the Act which would come into operation is July compensation would be paid from the day of the accident,and yet there were some people who said there was no difference between Toryism and Liberalism. Another important matter was the compensation for boys Under the old Act a lad earning 25. a day, if he were per- manently incapacitated received 6s.a week compensation. Now however,he had to come tip for revision at different ages when the amount of compensation would be in- creased in proportion to what his earning power would have been if he had not been injured. (Applause.) Compensation had also been extended to domestic servants and moreover the treatment of illegitimate children, which had long been a mockery, had been altered and eow the illegitimate child received the same protection as the legitimate child. There they had the difference between Toryism and Liberalism and how a workingman in face of that could be a Tory passed his comprehension. (Applause.) OLD-AGE PENSIONS. . Referring to the question of old-age pensions Mr. Harvey said he pledged them the old-age question was going to be dealt with. (Applause.) And not in the spirit of charity. That idea of bowing to the parson has gone out, and that idea of blankets and soup. (Laughter.) He believed workingmen and poor people should have blankets and soup, but he believed they should have them as a right and not as a charity. (Applause.) So they were going to solve that problem. People wanted to I, now where the money was coming from. There were streams of wealth to be tapped in this country. He was in favour of a graduated income tax. He believed a man that had ought to pay. (Applause.) Some of them would be able to show sources of revenue that could be utilized with advantage, to the benefit of the nation and the men who had helped to produce the wealth of the country. All wealth capital was accumulated labour. Hoarded wealth was wrong.</p>
        <p>CURE A COLD IN ONEDAY Take Laxative Bromo-Quinine Tablets. All Chemists refund the money if It fails. E. W. Grove's name on each box. is. lid</p>
        <p>There had never been equal distribution. Labour had ; not had its proper share.</p>
        <p>TETE EDUCATION QUESTION. In regard to the Education question they as Noncon- formists wanted no favour.(Hear, hear). They asked no favour and wanted nothing which was not given to every- one else. Years ago he protested against teachers being asked a question as to their religions opinions and teachers tests must be abolished. They must have popular control. The people who found the money had a right to call the tune. (Applause). He appealed to them to think seriously. Politics was not a game but if they acted justly and loved mercy politics would help them to get that which was their right and which belonged to them. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>INTERNATIONAL ARBITRATION</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONAL ARBITRATION</p>
        <p>which was making progress just as surely as arbitration in industrial conflicts. (Applause.) He had never heard a more eloquent and splendid speech than the Premier delivered to the representatives of those different nations. Most of the work in the House was serious enough but it was not all serious, for there was a great sense of humour there. Mr. Crosfield proceeded to give illustrations, the first being the case of Mr. Lupton who rose at 2.30 in the morning, when most members were feeling tired and wanted to get to bed, to discourse upon vaccination. However, they istened for a few minutes and then Mr. Lupton chanced to observe: "I suppose anybody who begins to talk about vaccination at this time of the morning,is looked noon either as a bore or an idiot." Perhaps much to his surprise there were loud cries of " Agreed." (Laughter). Referring to what had happened in War- rington during the last two years,Mr.Crosfleld said they had had the good fortune to be visited by a number of very able speakers. When he was invited to fill the very responsible position of candidate, he made up his mind,that however deficient in speaking he himself was, there should not be lack of good instruction :Ltd political addresses in the town, and he thought all would agree that they had been happy, largely through the influence of his good friend Mr. flowes—(applause)—in the number of able speakers who had visited them and in the very fair pro- portion of representatives of Labour included amongst them. (Applause.) They had been proud to have them, but he was sure that none would be welcomed with more pride and pleasure than Mr. Harvey, the newly elected member for North-East Derbyshire—(applause) —who would be one of the finest speakers in the House of Commons. (Applause.) Mr. Crosfield alluded to the splendid efforts made by the ladies of Warrington who had joined the Women's Liberal Association and swelled it to numbers far beyond anything they bad ever had before in the town. (Applause.) He thought that was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>MOST HOPEFUL SIGN</head>
        <p>A MOST HOPEFUL SIGN</p>
        <p>and he was sure all the men present would greatly appreciate the publi3 spirit which had prompted that political backing they had received from their lady friends. Another matter of importance was the development they had had among the Junior Liberals— (applause)—who by their initiative and with the advice and assistance of Mr. Howes had done such fine work, and who evidently did not mean to let the grass grow under their feet now or in the future. (Applause). Politicians could not afford to hide their light under a bushel and he did one little stroke off his own bat, for when they M were starting the big fight he said to r. , Howes : We have got splendid staunch supporters among the middle class in our town, but after all we are fighting for the good of the whole nation. We still are as Liberals in Warrington in a very special degree fighting THE 13,1 T ['LE OF INTELLIGENT LIROuR, _ .</p>
        <p>and I think intelligent labour should take the action into their own hands." (Applause). He suggested to Mr. Howes that it would be well if they formed that great association which they now had in Warrington, separate from anything like control by party officials, absolutely independent, including in its ranks Radicals and Liberals of all shades, having at heart the good and the advancement of Labour interests, and that they should call it the Progressive Labour League. (Applause). That League was certainly one of the greatest forces that contributed to the victory which they NVOD and he respectfully hoped and firmly believed that the organization would help their party and the progressive cause to hold the fort which they had won. With regard to</p>
        <p>THEIR OPPONENTS he would only say one word. If one went to the House of Commons he could not help feeling very sorrry for them. (Laughter.) As to the Government front bench, the number of men of ability who occupied it con- stituted such a galaxy of Went that they were almost tumbling over each other's heels. (Laughter.) When they looked to the other side, who was there? He admitted that as a dialectician and a tactician Mr. Balfour was a man of very great ability and one who was very courteous in the House,who had charming manners, and was very popular. He (Jr. Crosfleld) would be very sorry to say an unkind word about the ex-Premier, because he knew him years before he (the speaker) went into the House and he had been courteous and kind to him. Of course, political views did not make any diffczence when one got into the tea room or the smoke room. (Laughter.) From the Statesman's point of view, he had never agreed with Mr. Balfour, and he was I bound to say that during the last year he agreed with I him less than ever. He (Nfr. Crosfield) had referred to the Education Bill and one must speak frankly as to what one felt about such a disastrous mistake as Mr. Balfour made in that matter. Now a week since they bad him apparently swallowing the whole gospel of tariff reform and asking us, amongst other things, to tax ourselves up</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
