        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Madden.</head>
        <p>Madden.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was well filled on Monday evening when a mass meeting promated by the tVarrington Liberal Association was held. Considerable interest was taken in the proceedings as Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., attended to give an accaunt of the work already accomplished by the Governna nit and another of the principal speakers was Mr. W. E. Harvey, the popular Liberal Labour victor of North East Derbyshire. Prior to the meeting an excellent musical programme was submitted by Messrs. Crosfield's prize band. There was a large and enthusiastic audience and the meeting was a highly successful one. Mr. C. E. P trker presided and amongst others present on the platform were :—Mr. A. H. Crosaeld. M.P., Mr. W. E. Harvey, M.P., Mr. W. Madden, Mr. J. Howes, the Revs. J. Yonge and C. L. Stowe, Alderman Pierpoint, C nancillors Evans, Wilkinson, Ellison, Hawthorn, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, P. C. Garnett, E. Warren, Thomas Locker. W. Roberts, W. Furness, W. Bibby, John Maginn, E England, Thomas Penaberton, G. Walker, Felix Magian, Thomas Barnes (president .of the Progressive Labour Leagne), J. Morton (hon. secretary), .T. Howes, jour., ; Mesdanaes Parker. Holmes, Osborne, Dutton, Simpson, J. Maginn, F. Maginn, P. C. G-arnett, and Furness. The CHNIRMA.N announced that letters of apology for non-attendance had been received from Mr. Rigby Armitage, Mr. Henry Rlberts, and Nliss 13ess1e Dutton. Continuing, he congratulated Mr. Crosfleld on the inauguration of what he trusted would become a custom for the Member for Warrin;ton—to meet his constituents annually in that way. It was a nice and fitting thing to meet at the respective political clubs in connection with smoking concerts and so on—(laughter)—but he thought the whole constitu- ency had a right at least once a year to have the ' presence of the Member and that he should on such an occasion give an account of his stewardship. He hoped ; that meeting would begin a custom and that whoever their Member might be. or to whichever party he might belong, the doors of Parr Hall would at least once a year be thrown open and all the electors,irrespective of party, invited to come and hear the Member. They were very glad to have Mr. Crosdeld with them and to see him in what he (the chairman) believed to be greatly restored health. (Applause).</p>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECEI.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCkTION QUESTION AND TfIE HOUSE</p>
        <p>OF LORDS.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, who had a very cordial reception, said he was sure the audience would be kind and con- siderate because, although he was afraid he had done a great deal of "stumping" in Warrington in his time, that was the first occasion on which he had ever attempted to give a resume of the political work of the year and indeed it was rather a large order. (Hear, hear). When he was preparing his notes he had occasion to write Mrs. Crosfield at Colwyn Bay and he said that if she would allow him to quote a good old Lancashire expression, he felt much like a man who was trying to get a quart into a pint pot. (Laughter). He would, however, condense as well as he could what had taken place. He proposed first of all to deal with the measures they had had brought before thorn in Parliament and then to refer to some of the worx in Warrington during the last year or two. He heartily agreed with what Mr. Parker had said as to the advisability of their making that meeting an , annual custom. They had had other meetings in War- rington during the year which he had attended and they had one interesting gathering outside Warrington at which he made a sort of half-yearly report on Parliamentary proceedings. But he supposed the allure- ' mentsand the various attractions of the sands at South- port were too much for politics—(laughter)—and there was not a very large attendance at the political meeting which they held on that occasion. In any CaS3 that was only an interim report and he suggested to Mr. Howes thai it might be well if they held a meeting immediately the autumn session was over. However, Parliament continued its work so near to Christmas that he thought it was better that the meeting should be deferred to the present time. He would proceed to answer for his short-comings and perhaps afterwards he might undergo the purifying ordeal of a little of their criticism, for judging from a leaflet which had been placed in his hands, there might be some friendly criticism on the part of those who were present. (Laughter,) fie was reminded of a man who occupied the same position as he (Mr. Crosfleld) was in that night. He was addressing his constituents and said: " I have many short-comings. Probably all of you have short-comings. I don't imagine there is any single person hero who has yet met with a perfect man." One of the audience interrupted and said " Excuse me, but I have." " Indeed " said the speaker "and who was he ? " "Oh, well" was the reply. "He was my wife's first husband." (Loud laughter.) During the great battle they fought in the constituency, con- tinued Mr. Crosfield, there was one measure which, next to the question of Free Trade and Education—perhaps he might rank it with those questions—was pressed upon him very seriously and constantly, and he was thankful to say that that great and important measure had been dealt with and passed by both Lords and Commons. He was alluding to</p>
        <p>THE TRA.DES DISPUTES BILL. (Applause.) Lawyers had a great deal to say in the debates upon that Bill; employers and employed took a great interest in it throughout the country ; and he was afraid that some of his friends in Warrington in the Chamber of Commerce were not altogether satisfied with his attitude upon that question. While there were, he admitted, public questions on which they found so much to be said on both sides that it was sometimes difilcult to hit the happy mean, he had no misgiving whatever about a measure which he honestly and firmly believed would tend to industrial peace. (Hear, hear.) Ho would not discuss details which no doubt were familiar to them. Taking the broad view of it, he was perfectly satisfied that all responsible Labour leaders in this country were beyond any other people reluctant to force disputes to the arbitrament of strikes and trade warfare. (Applause.) They know well enough how serious that might be for capitalists but they also knew it was a still more serious thing for those who felt the pinch of hunger, the suffering and the misery which strikes entailed, and he called that a good enough protection for capitalists. He considered that Cipital was stronger than Labour and that the Goverment was absolutely bound in justice to the workingmen to put them in the position they held prior to certain judge-made law. He confessed that, sitting beside Mr. Shackleton during the debate in the Lords, he listened with admiration to the way in which the Lord Ckancellor piloted that great measure to a successful issue. (Applause.) He (Mr. Crostield) was speaking to a very large employer of labour in the House of Commons and he thought he summed up the whole matter in a very happy and pithy phrase. He said: "If I unfortunately should have any difference with my workpeople I would rather thrash it out, with them in, the open street than attack them on the back stairs." (Applause.) Then they had the extension of the Workmen's Compensa- tion Act. now including agricultural labourers, seamen and one or two other classes, which had been another measure of the very first importance passed by this Parliament. A third important Bill dealt with shipping, and most ably did Mr. Lloyd George— (applause)—whose nume was not forgotten nor likely to be forgotten by any of them—pilot that Bill through the House of Commons. A very noteworthy step had been taken by Mr. Sydney Buxton—(applause) —in recognizing trade unionism in the postal service. (Applause.) Another smaller measure that had been passed, and which was very important in its way, was the Housing and Allot- ments Act for small holdings in Ireland—a measure which they would certainly see extended in this country, so that there would be a real opportunity for agricul- tural labourers to Require a little property of their own. That concerned all town dwellers very much indeed, because it was the best possible means of stopping the constant flow of our population to the towns, and giving men some interest and ambition in their own sphere that would keep them in the country districts. (Applause.) The great struggle they had had during the year was over the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EDIICATrON BILL</head>
        <p>EDIICATrON BILL</p>
        <p>and to the very last moment opinion in the House was divided as to whether, he was going to say the House of Lords would throw it out, but he would rather say</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MR. W. MADDEN AND EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>MR. W. MADDEN AND EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. MADDEN, who had an enthusiastic reception, sup- ported the resolution, and said he took his stand on that platform as fearlessly as he did at the election. In dealing with the Irish Catholic voters in this town, Mr. Crosfleld had played the part of a straightforward and honourable man. (Applause.) He had seen nothing in his conduct in the course of the Education Bill to pre- vent him (Mr. Madden) from giving him in the future his own individual support and calling upon his brave countrymen and co-religionists to rally round him again and carry him to the front if it were necessary to do so. (Applause.) Where were their Tory friends ? "It appears that all they can do is to issue a miserable handbill of this kind" said Mr. Madden, waving a handbill in the air, 'something in the style and manner in which they con- ducted the election when they put false placards on the walls and stole our colours and did everything of a mean description. They are challenged here to-night to come and repeat before Mr. Crosfleld these sranders which they have the meanness to issue on this handbill." (Applause).Mr.MADDß.ti entered upon a lengthy criticism of the House of Lords and said he was not in favour of rosewater methods of dealing with that assembly. The people of the 20th century could not have such an anomaly,such an unholy and powerful growth, nurtured and fostered on privilege and when the people rose in their majesty could anything be found to be said in its defence? (Applause). The resolution was enthusiastically approved. MORE ABOUT THE LORDS. OLD AGE PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. CitOSPIELD in returning thanks said he had tried during the year to do simply what he promised to in the course of the election campaign. He told them that whatever questions came before Parliament while he was a member he should fael himself unfit to stand before his fellow-townsmen if to the extent of one six- pence he put his interests as a capitalist before what he thought was for the good of the country. That was the motive which had animated him and would continue to I animate him in his Parliamentary work. Mr. Crosfield expressed his gratitude to several prominent members of the Cabinet who had been kind enough to lend a helping hand and a word of advice to a new- comer. He was also indebted to Major Seely's information on the Chinese question, because through his good nature he had been able to keep in touch a little with Mr. Cresswell in South Africa. Mr. Cresswell had fought an uphill battle pluckily and honestly and he thoroughly deserved the support he had received from the House of Commons. Before the election he (Mr. Crosfleld) assured his constituents, ' that he would esteem it the greatest honour to have the friendship and advice of the Liberal Labour members, and he took the present opportunity of saying that he was full of gratitude for the patientand generousassist- ance given him time after time chiefly by the members who had been to Warrington to address them—Mr. Maddison, Mr. Vivian and Mr. Charles Fenwick who, he was happy to say, thanks to Mr. Howes, was going to pay them a visit. In all the com- plex legislation that was ahead of the Government on Labour and other questions he was thankful he was able to look forward to the same assistance from those gentlemen. (Applause.) With regard to the House of Lords his firm belief was that the initial step would not be a reform of the constitu- tion of that House. It would be, he believed, in the direction of making their veto suspensory. After that reform of the House itself would inevitably and naturally follow. He was well aware of the strength of the arguments which Mr. John Morley and others had advanced on behalf of a single Chamber, but they must take the country as it was, and the country as a whole was in favour of a two Chamber system. He (Mr. Crosfleld) must say he thought there were strong argu- ments for it at certain times, say in the crisis of a war fever when they might get hasty judgments by the popular Chamber checked by a second Chamber, if it were a good one. With regard to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>OLD AGE PENSIONS</head>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS</p>
        <p>he was truly thankful that what Mr. Harvey said was true, and that the -Government was going to do what had been talked of for so long. It seemed to him that just as they bad so often said that housing and taxation of land values were inextricably bound up, so they might make a trio of them, and by taxation of land values they would be able indirectly to solve the housing problem better than in any other way, and raise revenue which would contribute towards the cost of old age pensions. He believed it was hoped that not only would he follow the able leaders they had in Parliament, but that ho would take a prominent part himself. Perhaps a couple of years ago he had a little ambition in that direction, but last year his health was not as good as it was the year before, and he had had to be content to plays minor part. Perhaps he might have to continue it, but even so they had plenty of leaders, and his chief object which he hoped he would carry out conscien- tiously was to sift all the bi questions that came up, get all the advice he could on them, and use his judg- ment with care and discretion in coming to a decision. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>SUNDAY CLOSING.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY CLOSING.</p>
        <p>Questions were invited, but only one was sent up : "Is Mr. Crosfield in favour of a Sunday Closing Bill being passed without the electorate being consulted ?" Mr. CROSFIELD replied that if he had to give an answer" Yes "or " No," he would say "No." He had had some discussion on the question and he was con- vinced that the hours were too long. He would not like to see anything done over the heads of those engaged in the trade, but he would be willing to meet them, and recommend in their own interests, as well as in the interests of the public, that the hours of Sunday trading should be decidedly shortened. He would not commit himself to any specific number of hours without seeing what was proposed in the House but be would consider himself at liberty to vote for the hours being somewhat shortened.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>Our r-eaders' attekion is particularly directed to the first column on our front pag3, where a large number of coming events and Sunday services are advertised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Personal.</head>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Councillors Evans and Wilkinson and Messrs. William Furness and Willliam Bibby have been appointed delegates from the Warrington Liberal Association to attend the meeting of the National Liberal Federation Ex.scutive Committee, to be held at Doncaster on March Ist. A meeting in the even- ing will be addressed by Lord Crewe, Mr. Ackland, president of the Federation, and Mr. Nicholson, MY " HERALD " KITCREN RANGES and " Peveril " Fire Grates are hard to best.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, 13‘idge St., Warrington.—Advt. Church Society Addresses.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded congregation at the Parish Church last evening, when the Rev. Paul Bull, of Mirfield, delivered an address on" The life of man," in the course of which he paid a warm tribute to General Buller, with whom be served as chaplain. Next Friday the Rev. John Wal:eford will speak on "What is truth?" Male Voice Choir Concert.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Male Voice Choir will give their sixth annual-concert in the Parr Hall,to-night, when a first class entertainment is promised.ln addition to the Choir,selectirms will be given by Madame Jessie Hulme (soprano), Mr. Walter Lawley (tenor), Mr. Albert Garcia (baritone), Miss Jennie Parry (harpist), Mr. E. Bennett North (pianist), and Mr. Edwin Davies (humorist). Particulars as to prices will b 3 found on page 1. PIII3LIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEiT BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Ragged School and Mission. On Sunday evening the speaker at the lantern service, was Mr. Blakemore. The subject was," Lead us not int') temptation " and the slides were dearly described. Mr. Harry Wright recited "Trouble in the amen corner," A memorial service was then held for one of the late scholars, Miss E. Savage. At the close the organist played, "The dead march in Saul " the congregation standing. On Monday evening a splendid concert was given by the members of the limelight committee which attracted and pleased about 400 people.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Teachers' Association. The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Sunday School Teachers' Association was held in St. Paul's Girls' School on Monday evening, when an address was given by the Rev. T. T.Lee-Jones, vicar of Dares- bury, on "Catechising." The Rev. W. Bracecamp presided. The lecturer said he was sure all the clergy present would back him up when be said that if there was one great asset in the work of a parish it was the asset of the Sunday school teacher. (Applause.) They turned out every Sunday afternoon to their work, and I there were none who deserved more support and gratitude.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages. Mr. Crosfield at the Liberal Club. Death of Mr. W. Atherton. Education Committee. Runcorn, Widnes, St. Helens &amp; Earlestown News Letters to the Editor. 3. Football. Women's Realm. Short Story. Votes for Women. 6. Rev. John Yonge on the New Theology. Druid's Presentation. Children's Corner. 7. Notes on Games and Athletics. Captain Crosfield on" England's Danger. 'f Elective Auditors' Election. Thursday was the day appointed for the nomination of candidates for the positions of elective auditors for the borough. The two retiring gentlemen, Mr. E. England and Mr. Laurence Bolton were again nomin- ated and as no new candidates were brJught forward, they are returned unopposed. Messrs. Richmond's New Works The extensive new premises of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd. near Latchford Locks were opened this week. At the present time the works, which are of a most up-to-date character, are only partially occupied but by a gradual process the busi- ness will be transferred from Academy-street to Latchford. We purpose giving, a full description, next week, of the new buildings. UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.— Popular Penny Concert. • There was a crowded attendance at the popular penny concert at 80ld-street Wesleyan Schoolroom on Saturday evening, when an excellent programme arranged by Mr. Whitfield, was given. Songs were rendered by the Misses Violet Brown and Miss Barnes and Messrs. J Bradshaw and H. Dauber. A humorous duet, entitled "A.8.C." by Miss Alice Taylor and Master Thomas Allender, was greatly appreciated, Miss S. Clatworty and Miss Thompson gave several mandoline and banjo solos in an able manner. The humorist was Mr. D. Sutton, a Lancattire dialect reciter from Ashton, who caused much laughter. STRANGE'S A.l. CrvstalLamP OIL in barrels or drums delivered free.—So le agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge St., Warrington.--Advt. Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Pace.</p>
        <p>There was a fair attendance at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A.at Wycliffe Church on Sunday. Coun- cillor Hawthorn presided, and said although their numbers were not as large as they were fourteen years ago, they were to be congratulated upon the work Which had been done under the guidance of the Presi dent and the very efficient officers of the society. Mr. Johnson, of Manchester, spoke upon the greatest ques- tion of the day "Christ lifted up upon the Cross." He urged the brethren to intelligently and emotionally look to the man Christ, and the experiences of life, which would reveal to them more and more the great realities of Christ's great saving power. (Applause.) Mr. Barnes sang " Rock of 4.ges " and "Crossing the Bar." Mr. Garner and Mr. Healey also took part in the meeting. WOOD CH ENINEIT PIECES, Fire Grates and Tiles unrivalled seletffon, bispectioct invited.—J. S. G IRLA ND, Builders' Ironmonger, ctc., 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—.Advt. " Y " Branch B W T.A.</p>
        <p>The " " Branch N.13--.W.- .T.A.., held their Frances Willard Memorial Evening on Wednesday last in Silver-street schools. Previous to the meeting tickets had been given to the girls of Cockhedge Mill inviting them to a pleasant meeting. Miss Dutton presided over a very good meeting which opened with a hymn and prayer, after which Miss Farrar gave a pianoforte solo. Miss Dutton spoke a few words, and after a solo from Miss Haviour, introduced the speaker, Rev-. J. F. Matthews.—Mr. Matthews, in a few well-spoken word, full or both pathos and humour, urged the girls who had not already done so to sign the pledge, and with God's help to keep it. Twenty-six girls res- ponded to the call and signed, and as each did so Mr. Matthews witnessed them. Miss Cox and Miss Black-. stook then gave solos, and Miss Wilson a recitation, after which the meeting closed with the favourite hymn "God be with you till we meet again." TABLE, FLOOR, AND HANGING LAMPS in large variety. Latest patterns.—J. S. GARLA.ND, Iron- monger Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Shorthand Writers' Association. There was a moderate attendance at the weekly meeting of the above Association held at the Winmar- leigh Cafe on Wednesday. Speed practice at varying rates was conducted, and during an interval the result of the Shorthand Competition held last week was announced. and Mr. A. N. Legge, M.1.P.5., presented the prizes to the respective winners—Miss Connolly, and Mr. J. Walsh. Mr. Legge then gave an instruc- tive lecture on the " Causes of Phonographic Failure." Ile regretted that so many students commenced the study of shorthand, and after a very short time abandoned it as being "too hard." In his opinion, method, practice and patience would overcome many of the suppcsed difficulties. The lecturer went thoroughly into the question of why so many would-be shorthand writers could not correctly read their notes, and gave numerous appropriate illustrations on the 4 blackboard in support of his remarks. He also laid great stress on the fact that a good shorthand writer must needs possess a good vocabulary. Several of the members complimented Mr. Legge on his interest- ing paper, and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded him.</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Vaudeville Entertainments at the Parr Hall. Warrington people will be interested to hear that Vaudeville entertainments are to be given at the Parr Hall every night next week. Unusually attractive programmes are promised, excellent musical talent having been engaged. This new departure will be under the management of Mr. H. E. Barnes, who is associated with the new theatre, which is being erected off Barbauld-street, and as he is on trial, so to speak, as an entertainer in Warrington, we have no doubt he will present a first-class entertainment. There will be two houses nightly, and popular prices are announced. Among the artistes advertised to appear are the celebrated Boccacios in their musical melange, introducing Signor Francia, the champion mandolinist of the world. Signor Francia has taken part in com- petitions all over the world for the past eight years, and was last year presented with a shield by the Queen of Italy. The shield is hung in the Musicians Academy in Milan. Signor Francia has comnosed a special march in honour of Mr. Barnes, which he will personally conduct on the orchestra during the week. The other artistes who are particularised in our advertising' columns include comediennes, dancers, cartoonists, vetriloquists and instrumentalists of the highest class. We hope the Warrington public will cordially support Mr. Barnes in the first of his local ventures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>PUBLIC BIEVEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BBST BOOTS IN LANCASFIIRE.—AdVt. Warrington Field Club.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC BIEVEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BBST BOOTS IN LANCASFIIRE.—AdVt. Warrington Field Club.</p>
        <p>Mr. ,Jan Fairhurst presided over the meeting held in the Museum Lecture Room, on Friday evening, when Mr. Charles White read a paper "Notes on the Ice Age." Dealing with the climatic changes that followed the close of the Tertiary period, he stated that glacial conditions prevailed over large areas in the northern hemisphere for many thousands of years. Though the duration of the ice age was uncertain there seemed good reason to think that it lasted for quite 400,000 years. He remarked that many guesses had been made at the cause, but whatever the cause there was no doubt that the climate of Britain, of Europe and of North America, which had been tropical in the late Tertiary period, gradually became arctic or sub- arctic in the centuries following its close. The fauna and flora were correspondingly modified, many plants and animals disappearing altogether. The lecturer then gave a picturesque description of the areas which were subject to glaciation and of the physical conditions of the earth generally during tke ice age, with special reference to the work of glaciers. Mr. White concluded with a brief allusion to the many speculations as to the cause of the ice age and an expression of belief that it was not an isolated phenomenon, but that there had been similar periods at earlier stages of the world's history. A long and interesting discussion followed, Mr. White being warmly congratulated on his lucid presentation of a complicated subject.</p>
        <p>A GUARANTEED CURE FOR PILES, Itching, Blind, Bleeding or Protruding. All chemists are authorised to refund money if Pazo Ointment fails to cure Piles. Cures ordinary cases in six days. One application gives ease and rest. Paso Ointment is a new discovery, and the only Pile remedy sold on a posi- tive guarantee. Price 2s. 3d., of all chemist or from,— f'ARIS MEDICINE Co., TEMPLE CHAMBERS, LONDON, E.C.</p>
