        <p>doctrine of the immanence of God in nature and all T. Lowe l6 T. Lowe Jones Jones 73 E. Robinson Humanity. 'they learnt from the Bible that God was omnipresent; He was everywhere and in Him we lived and : 666 moved and had our being. They were in His hands at that ! moment, each breath they drew was in Him but that, PADGATE v. ST. CLEMENTS, Playel at Padgate February 14th. Score : did not mean that they were God. The astoundingly im- PIDGATE. IST. ST. CLEMENTS IST. perfect doctrine of the New Theology ignored altogether J. Weston W. Bowering . 94 J. Fleming the transcendence of God.lt ignored Hie Infinite differ- 75 P. H Simkin ence in nature and character from his human offspring " g J. Roberts 75 R. Woods and that was to fail in reverence and humility. When C. Roberts 59 J. Harrison they were told that God was man and that man was W. Weston 65 P. Brimelow God—that the two were essentially the same—whatever R. Hanablett 44 J. Wolfeuden the speakers meaning, the phraseology savoured both of i.reverence and impossibility but it explained how the i 352 new theology could affirm that every men was a possible Majority for St. Clements Ist 51 points. : Christ. Let them note another sequence of this Played at Barik-street. Score incongruous and impossible doctrine—how inevitably it I „- - Sr. CLEMENT'S 2ND. PADTATE 2ND. affected the idea and estimate of sin. The New ,H. Broadhurst H. C. Rhoden 75 J. Davies J. Testament spoke of the exceeding sinfulness of sin. It 57 G. Faulks said that the" waves of sin is death "—that sin was so F. Sharpe 44 H. Ratcliff infinitely guilty that its removal and blotting out P. Ha.nkinson W. Holmes necessitated the Incarnation and death of the Son of 75 H. Smith God. The new theology however told them that sin A. G.Honkins 72 It. Turner was selfishness. Surely that was an absolutely inadequate • and unspeakably misleading representation. Well did! Dr. Garvie say "sin is something more and worse— unless wa give the term selfishness a far greater moral content than is usually given it. Human vice,so shame- ful that one blushes to think of it, human crime so terrible that one shudders to think of it human bondage to sin so helpless that its victims have been driven to des- pair, are not adequately described as seltishness."The new theology did not cast its plummet deep enough into the abysmal depths of man. The new creed about sin would not do. It broke down before the facts of human nature and character. The difference in the nature and character between man and God could never in that way be truthfully minimized. The idea that it could must surely come from failure to apprehend the august and unapproachable perfection of God on the one hand, and the intense and evil char- acter of sin on the other. The great expounder of the New Thsology had given another and still more surpris- ing definition of sin—a definition so utterly startling that one almost shrank from quotation but in order to show the extravagances towards which the new doctrine apparently led the quotation might be justified: " Sin itself is a quest for God—a- blundering quest, but a quest for all that. To explain his meaning Mr. Camp- bell said The man who got dead drunk last night eid so because of the impulse within him to break through the barriers of his limitations, to express himself and to realize the more abundant life. His self-indulgence just came to that : he wanted if only for a brief hour to live the larger life, to expand the soal, to enter untrodden regions and gather to himself new experi- ences. That drunken debauch was a quest for life, a quest for God. Men in their simple follies to-day and their blank atheism and their foul blasphemies, their trampling upon things that are beautiful and good are engaged in this dim blundt.ring quest for God whom to know is life eternal. The roue in Piccadilly last night who went out to corrupt innocence and to wallow in the filthiness of the flesh, was engaged in his blunder- ing quest for God." He (Mr. Yonge) ventured to sug- gest that the whole Bible from Genesis to Revelations uttered an absolute contradiction of any such extenua- tion and misrepresentation of sin. The sinner such as the paragraph quoted delineated knew no care for God —no desire or thirst for Him. Sin was more than selfishness, more than a blunder. It was the parent of vice, violence and crime. It had been the prolific and time-long Cause of all the misery and wretchedness of human nature and human history. It was the rebellion of man against his God and any theology which made light of it or which in any degree under estimated its evil and guilt was an absolutely false guide to the souls of men. It would prove to any soul which was beguiled by it, a false beam playing and dancing upon tile mire of filth and evil and decoying unwary souls to death and ruin. Surely it was not too strong a view to take that a theology which taught that doctrine about sin put itself beyond the faith of any man who knew anything about the evil of his own heart. When they were tempted of evil they knew full well that their temptation was in no sense a blundering quest for Goda followed,naturally, therefore, that the New Theolocy under-estimated and misrepresented the need for the Atonement. If Mr. Campbell was to be accepted as the high priest of the New Theology the Cross of Christ stood to lose its accustomed and authorised place as "the power of God unto salvation." Despite the changing theories the Cross through the ages had shown the same mysterious redeeming power, but the New Theology went far to dethrone that power. Its eloq tient exponent poured con- tempt upon any idea of the vicarious sacrifice of the Cross. According to Mr. Campbell Christ's death was not something done for the world and yet the New Testmant again and again reiterated" Christ died for our sins." Nothing was easier in reference to the explicit New Testament words than to do with them as Mr. Campbell did amid the cheers of his audience dispose of them as the mere opinion of an Apostle who did not know so much of the mind of God as the advocates of the new theology. But it was questionable whether such cavalier methods could succeed. Was it not true that the power of Christianity had been the power of the Cross? Not the power of any theories of the Atone- ment but of the Love Divine of which the Cross was the transcendent exhibition. Had it not been proved over and over again that the matchless sacrifice had been the " power of God unto salvation " of the greatest and vilest sinaers tbe world had seen. He was there to affirm his conviction that the New Theology, however broad or philosophic, which withheld or obscured the Atoning Cross presented an emasculated gospel, and was doomed to failure and rejection. The words of Paul gave he key note to Christian preachers of that or any age—" I am not ashamed of the Gospel of Christ : God forbid that T should glory save in the Cross of our Lord Jesus Christ."</p>
