        <p>ST. HELENS L. SC N.-W. RECREATION CLUB V. WARRING TON L. &amp; N.-W. RAILWAY INSTITUTE •</p>
        <p>Played at St. Helens on Saturday, February 16th. Scores ST. HELENS. WA RR INGTON. E. Foreman ISO T. Horrabin W. Taylor A. Williams • 76 J. Jenkinson D. Morris C. Willis 7O G. Parker T. Wilson - 100 A. Pearson W. Sephton</p>
        <p>we have bought you a briar-root one with amber mouth- piece, silver-mounted with case, together with tobacco, which lam pleased to hand to you. And now let me say, the money value of these articles is not very con- siderable, you won't think the less of them for that, but I am sure that you will prize more than money the good wishes and esteem of your fellow members who have been inspired to do you the honour you so well deserve. (Loud applause.) The Lodge Secretary (Bro. WAt.Binnvl congratulated Bro. Barlow, and said that to form some idea of the length of time Bro. Barlow had been a Druid, that he was now beginning to feel an old Druid, and that Bro. Barlow had been a Druid almost twice as long as he had. It was such members as Bro. Barlow that had made the Druids Hope Lodge one of the best of any Friendly Society in Warrington, for they had a Management Fund alone worth nearly £7 per membsr,and that outside the Sick and Funeral benefits of the Lodge. Druids Hope was a unique Lodge, inasmuch that all the officers were of very long standing. Bro. Barlow had been Senior Trustee for a great many years ; Bro. Fodmore bad been Treasurer for over 37 years ; Bro. Woods the Steward for over 30 years ; and he himself had been Secretary for over 30 years. (Applause). The District Secretary (Bro. E. ALLEN) said Bro. Barlow had attained an age as a Druid that no other prominent member of the Order in Warrington had ever attained, and he was proud to be able to congratulate him. (Applause). The District Grand Master Bro. G. H. Houghton, the D.D.G.NI. Bro. Jackson, D. T. Bro. R. Perdue, P.D.G. Ms Bro. Roughley, Packer, Crosskill, Massey, Woods, John Bibby, Bros. Hewitt, Shaw and the N.3.A. Bro. Cook also congratulated Bro. Barlow. Bro. BARLOW rising to respond was received with enthusiastic cheering said that he had intended being present at that the sixtieth anniversary of his being made a member of the Order of Druids, but had not the faintest idea that he would have been the recipient of such kind words and good wishes and such a beautiful present. He very feelingly thanked everyone for their expressions towards him and the memory of that meeting would ever be in mind. He did not know that he deserved such things for he bad only tried to do his little for the welfare of the Order, he had certainly been in office almost the whole of his GO ye‘rs member- ship and he thought he was just simply doing his duty to his fellow members as every other member should. (Loud applause.) A vote of thanks to the District Officers for their presence ended the meeting. The silver rose bowl was supplied by Messrs. Eustance, Sankey-street and are on view together with Bro. Barlow's first contribution card dated 1817 in Messrs. Dolan's window, Horsernarket-street.</p>
