      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>BILE —7 EANSfor ILIOUSNESS</head>
        <p>BILE —7 EANSfor ILIOUSNESS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S OLDEST DRUID.</p>
        <p>Interesting Presentation.</p>
        <p>At the usual monthly meeting of the Druids Hope Lodge No. 4GI of the Order of Druids, Warrington District, held on Tuesday evening in the Druids Hall, a very interesting presentation took place. The N.G.A. Bro. R. Cook presided and was supported by the D.G.I. Bro. G. H. Houghton, D.D.G.M. Bro. John Jackson,D.P. Bro. R. Perdue, D.C.S. Bro. E. Allen, P.D.G.As. Bros. G. Roughley, S. Packer, John Woods, James Massey, William Crosskill and John Bibby, V.G.A. Bro. T. Hewitt, Lodge Treasurer, Bro. Joseph Podmore, Lodge Secretary Bro. William Bibby and a good attendance of members. During the evening P.D.G.51. Bro. James Massey delighted the meeting with his elocution in the Lancashire and Yorkshire dialects. After the ordinary lodge business had been transacted the N.G.A. called upon the Lodge Treasurer Bro. Joseph Podmore to make the presentation. Bro. PODMORE said: It has fallen to my lot to do to-night what to me is a very pleasing duty, that is, to offer in the name of the members of this Lodge our hearty congratulations to our respected brother William Barlow on his attaining his sixtieth birthday as a mem- ber of the Druids' Hope Lodge of the Order of Druids. Our minute book tells us that he was admitted a member of this Lodge on the third Tuesday in February, 1847, GO years ago, the third Tuesday in the month being the night fixed upon for our monthly meeting when the Lodge was first opened and from which we have never changed. Now I venture to say that there are very few members in any Friendly Society who are privileged to reach so long a membership as our brother, and I tnay say this also, that there are very few who have taken so deep an interest in their Society's welfare as Bro. Barlow has in the Order of Druids. lam saying this after an experience of 52 year's membership with him in the same Lodge, and when I look back at the work of the Order I have found him ever willing to take office when required and always eager to advance the interest of our Society. He has filled many positions in our Order and the same can be said in respect to our own Lodge, and whilst ever willing to advance the interest of our Society by advice or work there is one branch of the Order which he has taken a special interest in—our Widows and Orphans Fund. I remember that when this Fund was established he along with others, who have been called to rest, took a very active part in its formation with a determination to make it a success, and that success has been achieved, for after paying every claim against us and giving grants at different times we have nearly one thousand pounds in hand. I am proud to 4ay that Bro. Barlow is Treasurer to that fund and has been since its formation and we members have such confidence in him that we re-elect him year after year. I know his ambition is that the fund may reach one thousand pounds. May he live to see the desire of his heart fulfilled. (Applause.) I hope the young men of our Order will take Bro. Barlow as their example, ever remembering that it is the young men who are the back hone of a Lodge—the plodders that make a society a success—the workers who have done noble work on behalf of their fellow men. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Podnaore then presented to Bro. Barlow in the name of the members of the Lodge, a silver rose bowl which bore the following inscription. " Presented to P.D.G.M. Bro. William Barlow on attain- ing his Diamond Jubilee as a member of the Hope Lodge No. 401 of the Order of Druids, Warrington District, Febroary 19th 1907." When we decided last Lodge night to oommemorate your Diamond Jubilee, our members knowing that you were an old veteran with the pipe gave strict orders that we must get you one, whatever form our other present might take. So following out their instructions</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The annual session of the South-West Lancashire. District Lodge was held on Saturday at the Dingle, Liverpool, being attended by some 230 representa- tives from aa. parts of the district. The visitors included the Crand Chief Templar (County Councillor Joseph Malins),!the Grand Vice-Templar (Mrs. Walker), and the G.A.S. (1340. J. Bardsley). The reports of the officers reviewed the work of the past year, Mr. R. D. Petterson, the retiring District Chief Templar, being heartily thanked for his services. Thirty-eight can- didates were raised to the district degree. The D.E.S. (Mr. G. S. Schofield, of Bottle) gave an excellent report on the electoral department, and the following resolu- tion was unanimously adopted : —" That, while thank- ing the Prime Minister and the Government for their promise of a bill on temperance lines, we would urge that it should include (1) a short time limit to the compensation clauses of the Act of 1904 ; (2) the restoration of the discretionary power of the licensing magistrates, and that every kind of licence should come under their control : (3) a direct veto power, unhampered and unhindered by any system of municipal, public or company management of liquor shops, be conferred on the i people in local areas to enable them to prevent, f they so desire, the issue or renewal of all licences for the sale of intoxicating liquor within their own districts" The representatives elected to the Grand Lodge were Messrs. R D. Pattersln, J. Oliver, Morris Jones, A. Miller and G. B. Walker, the officers el-wen for the ensuing year being :—D.D.T., Mr. J. Oliver ; D.Co., Mr. H. Hodgson; D.S.J.W., Ur. G. Clarke ; DES., Mr. G. S. Schofield ; D.V.T., Mrs. Quinton; D.Sec., Mr. M. Jones : DL-., Mr. G. B. Walker ; Mr. J, Parr ; D.M. Mr. A. Miller ; D.D.:11., J. Smith ; D. Sec., Mr. T. Lewis ; P.D.C.T., Mr. R. D. Petters ,n ; D.A.S. Bro. A. Francis; D. Messenger, Miss Fleming; Foreign Correspondent, Mr. Trampleasure ; D. Organist, Mr. C. Griffiths. These were duly installed by the G-.C.T. The next session will be held at Southport.</p>
      </div>
