      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EVER ORGIE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>A EVER ORGIE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At Newton last Thursday, Edward Murphy was ,charged with stealing 4s. 6d. from the dwelling-house of George Cosgrove, 78, Legh-street. Earlestown, last Friday.—Mr. Cosgrove deposed that Murphy was drink- ing with her husband on the night of Friday the Bth, and the morning of Saturday the 9th, and on the Satur- day morning she found that 4s. 6d. was missing which she had put into the box for her rent. She accused him of stealing it, sad he struck her several times.— Annie Penketh, living in the house, deposed to the drinking in the house on the Friday night, when Prisoner sent for three quarts of bear. On the Saturday morning he sent out for two more quarts for which Cosgrove lent him the money as he had none. He turned out his empty pockets. Later, she, at his request, went and pawned his vest, and more beer was -sAnt for. Then Mrs. Cosgrove found that her rent money was gone. Sho searched witness, and locked the door far a policaman to he sent for to search prissner. Prisoner had been left in the house alone while she went to pawn his waistcoat. Witness had none of the beer. Mrs. Cosgrove had ono cupful, and the two men drank all the rest.—Prisoner: It was my own meney, earned at Winwick. If I had told these people all the money I had they would have wanted rue to spend it all. I spent 3s. Bd. at their house for beer, as I hftvo spent many a pound there before. I have befriended the woman by buying her coal and food and naying her rent—Mrs. Cungrove, recalled, admitted that he had befriended her before. The Clerk: Why did you pawn your waistcoat? Prisoner: Because didn't want them to know I had any money. The Chairman said they were all so helplessly mixed up with beer that the Bench thought there was a doubt in the .case, which they dismissed.</p>
