      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NEW THEOLOGY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THEOLOGY.</p>
        <p>THE REV. JOHN YONGE'S CRITICISM. The Old is Bettor.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening, the Rev. John l'onge took for his subject "The New Theology, or the old : Which ?" Basing his remarks on Luke v. 39, " The pad is better," he said he did not take the words as a text, but because they gave a con- cise and summary answer to the question. His hope was that in that congregation might be found a very general acceptance of the verdict that of the two theologies "The old is the better." They had been witnessing lately what could be truthfully described as a very remarkable phenomenon. Within the last few weeks theology had assumed a prominence and popu- larity usually monopolised either by politics or the passing sensational events of the day. An eloquent and comparatively young preacher in London had attracted by his recent teachings the attention of all churches, creeds and classes. Anglican and Roman, Evangelical and Unitarian had all joined in discussing "the New Theology" as principally propounded by hi m.Widespread and even fierce controversy had a. isen,higotry and bitter- ness had found in some quarters unhappy resurrection, and even in their own Congregational ranks there had been aroused an intolerance and uncharitableness which were alien, not only to Christian for- bearance and charity but even to the distinctive doctrinal principles and liberties of Congrega- tionalism. They must not, however, be blind to the fact that there were aspects in that phenomenon which were distinctly of good and hopeful omen. It was at. lomat a positive good that the interest of the people generally in religious truth and doctrine should have been thus suddenly revived and increased. The man in the street was becoming interested and he was the very individual whom the churches had not as yet very signally attracted. To interest him might be, if oppert unity be wisely improved, to capture him and hence arose occasion and necessity to reaffirm the doctrines of the Gospel of Jesus Christ, and to makes new appeal to the intellect,the conscience and the heart ,of the people. The pulpit woull have to become more I definitely and intelligently doctrinal than of late. If the new doctrines were misleading they must be refuted by the clearer enunciation of the evangelical doctrines which were as old as the Bible, and as true as Jesus Chi ist. He did not think Christian people had any need i to tremble as though the Ark of God were now in any I danger. Its far greater peril had been in times of popular apathy and indifference. Whilst therefore they might be compelled to dissent from the new theology, they might be grateful to the minister of the City Temple that he had been the means of awakening and spreading so wide and powerful an interest in religions truth. Congregationalism allowed to all its constitu- ents entire freedom of thought, faith and speech. The utterances of any one man or of any number of men had no binding effect upon the belief of the community. Congregationalism admitted of divergencies of opinion; it had an open mind; it believed—aa one of its historic founders expressed—" That the Lord had yet more light and truth to break forth front His word." It remained entirely and solely the personal responsibility of Mr. Campbell as to what he should say. His audience would not endorse his views because they listened to them and their Churches were not going to be compromised by anything which Mr. Campbell or any other man might say or teach. It was an apostolic counsel which said : " Prove all things. Hold fast that which is good." Mr. Campbell repudiated the phrase" New Theology" as being incorrect and misleading. The views which he and some others professed were only new in the very limited sense that anantiquated dress might be regarded as new when after being hidden for age, the wheel of fashion brought it again to light.He the preacher would not labour the point further than to say that old as the " new " views were in reality they did not commend themselves as being Its old as Jeans Christ, and His Apostles. and consequently they we,e prepared to say that the old was better than the " new." The new theology would puzzle and distress ordinary minds by its definition of God. Asked" What is God ?" the new theolopy bewilderingly replied " God is Whoever or Whatever the Power behind phenomenon may be." God was " Whoever " or " Whatever." He or It. Another definition was that God "was the Infinite reality whence all things pro-cad." They were told further that the new theology, ia common with the whole scientific world,believed that the infinite universe " as one asnect or expression of the reality " but it thinks of " Him "or " It " as " conscioneness rather than blind force." It might be fairly asked whether the new theological definition of God was very nseful to the ordinary mind and whether it was likely to be a popular aid to the realization and comprehension of God? Would it help them to kueel down and pray? Would they be encouraged to call "The. Power behind phenomena," "The infinite reality whence all things proceed," " Our Father ?" When they read the Loiays own teaching did they not feel a responsive thrill in their inmost souls as He told them that " Our Father knows what things we have need of" and bid them when they prayed to say" Our Father which art in Heaven:They could realize what was God as they sat at the feet of Jesus and learnt of Him,but when they read the phrasing of the new theology definition of God they were befogged and mystified. Not only we,e they perplexed by the new theology soncerning God but they were plunged into similar mystification about Jesus Christ and also about them- selves. Concerning Jesus Christ the new theology made short and summary work with the supernatural birth at Bethlehem. Mr. Campbell told them We know that Jesus was the Son of a human father." Person- ally he (Mr. Yonge) did not think that the doctrine of the divine nature of Christ depended upon the fact of the Virgin birth although he had not yet seen reason to disbelieve that fact hut accepted it entirely. Certain, however, on the point himself the preacher of the City Temple had founded upon that belief the furtheir and remarkable assertion that "Every man was a potential (or possible) Christ ,—or rather a manifestation of the Eternal Christ—that side of the nature of God from which all humanity has come." Surely that declaration was even staggering, especially when it was combined with another from the same source to the effect that " Christ is Divine : and so are we." It could hardly he surprising that the town, thereofanthnd:ts vile in estatements should he regarded as very much like a Unitarian, because if all the people in the awnedrethepusworld, „ peovetenntthirei profligate, Christs then Christ Himself must have been as utterly and only human as any or all ; better indeed, purer. nobler, greater as might be the moral than the immoral, the clean than the foul, the upright than the dishonest—but essentially and completely and slily human as the lowest and the worst. If that was the New Theology then give him the old. That perplexing statement had evoked very numerous replies. Professor Garvie, ProiltreipealinofLothnedoir seamidier: theologionl Congregational c "To describe ourselves as potential Christs appears both ungrateful.s unillqfluned Hthnenmanci ty. both irreverent ond thi of the fact of the Virgin birth not a hindrance p acceptance ae Father, and ii sea thu da isat generally the e, ruaorinal moral admitted doh ma raiatlutoteneder, ctirtohenenvdseepcarei revealed ft daiesncntdtheii esonsngFoaof okh religious nfelecpw h rai nns td The including the moral conscience of Christ, in which human penitence and Divine pardon had no plane— compel me to connect His Person not with that side of the nature of God from which all humanity has coma forth but with God as transcending man in absolute perfection,And to claim equality with Hint upon whom I depend for life in God would be ungrateful." It had been pointed out that that statement of the New Theology paid no regard to Christ's own claim of absolute sinlessness. Therein Jesus was supremely differentiated from all humanity —even frprn the saintliest. How then could it be said "Every man is a pot ential Christ"or how could it be true to say"the love of Jesus is the same as the love of John Smith "? Jesus was not to be accounted for on any such hypothesis and although he becarne"the first born among many brethren" none of them could or would arrogate to themselves the spotless innocence of their Elder Brother. Those remarkable ideas had come from what seemed to be an exaggerated notion of the scriptural</p>
      </div>
