      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>MT VERY DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,-</head>
        <p>MT VERY DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,-</p>
        <p>In response to many kind inquiries after my health, I am glad to say that I have entirely lost the bad cold I had when I wrote last; but am—very,-very sorry to say that I have con- tracted another one. Well, although I have been a bit out of sorts myself, I hope all of you have been enjoying the best of health; and, judging by the cheering letters you have written, I should say you are Al. This week I have received two more contributions on "How I spent Christmas-day," 90 I think that next week I shall have to devote the whole of the Corner to this subject. The Post Cards are not over quick in coming in, my deans. Who'll be among the next 100 to send. one in? I hope some of you are not refraining from sending because you cannot find a P.C. with a funny verse or joke on it. If this is the case' make something up yourself. Now, I hope by next week to receive at least one hundred pretty cards, and this is not by any means a large quantity out of twelve or fourteen hundred re- lations, is it? UNCLE NED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WELCOME TO</head>
        <p>WELCOME TO</p>
        <p>Beatrice Cheesman, Evelyn Kinsley, 'Har- greaves Parkinson, Torn Taylor, John W. Smith.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am sending you a short contribution for the "Children's Corner." Hoping you will like it, and that you are keep- rug well. With best wishes to you and all my cousins, I remain, your affectionate Niece,</p>
        <p>HILDA MOUNCE.</p>
        <p>"NOT SMART ENOUGH."</p>
        <p>A gentleman once went into a refreshment- room, and told the manager he would give him tive-pound note if he would give him what he wanted. This is what, he asked for. The pride of a woman roasted. The best part of a man fried. Temptation under puffed paste. Sham agony without pain.</p>
        <p>Answer.</p>
        <p>Roaiated hare. Fried sole. Apple tart. Chainpagne.</p>
        <p>The manager could not guess what it was, so he did not have the money.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l was sorry to see in your last letter that you had a bad cold. I hope it is better now. I am sending you an essay on "How I spent Christmas-day." We are still having rather cold weather here, and we had a little snow last Sunday. Now I must say good-bye.</p>
        <p>From your ever-loving Niece, MARGARET MARY PRESS.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l thank you very much for that bottle of Mason's Extract. I am sending you a piece of poetry. It is entitled, "The Pony," which I hope to see in print:—</p>
        <p>THE PONY.</p>
        <p>If e'er I have a pony, Upon which I can ride, I never will neglect him, Whatever may betide; I'll ride him every day, And I'll make him very gay, So, trot, trot, trot, my pony, Trot, trot, trot, trot, trot away.</p>
        <p>I'll put him in a stable, And keep him nice and warm, To make him strong and able, My journey to perform. I'll feed him with sweet hay, And to him will eay, Come, trot, trot, trot, my pony, Come, trot, trot, trot, trot away.</p>
        <p>I will not overheat him, For that would make him weak, But. very kindly treat him, And keep his coat quite sleek; And then he'll never stray, But be steady when I say, Come, trot, trot, trot, my pony, Come, trot, trot, trot, trot away. A steady pace I'll teach him, For fear that he should slip; But, never will I hurt him 'With neither spur nor whip; But always to him say, In a most gentle way, Come, trot, trot, trot, my pony, Come, trot, trot, trot, trot away. GWENDOLINE KIVELL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>"ROYAL BOUNTY."</head>
        <p>"ROYAL BOUNTY."</p>
        <p>A king one day gave out that all who came to him should have what. they asked. o the noble and the rich begged for titles and lands, and some even asked tor silver and gold. iiut all they asked for, all they had. Then came the poor and the simple, who wanted a like boOn. "Ye come late," said the king, the noble and the rich have been here, and have taken my lands and wealth." At this the poor people were sadly troubled, and the king was sorry for them. "My friends, though I have parted with my wealth, I have still my power.. No one asked for that. I will make you all the judges and masters of the land."</p>
        <p>When this came to the ears of the rich, they went to the king, and said to the kxxL, My lord, we are sadly troubled tlt :o1 hould. appoint thee poor wretches to rul‘ over us,, it were better that we did not subn.i!</p>
        <p>"Sirs," said the king, "I do yi,u iio wiuldg; all that you asked I gave, so th.A.nought was left to me save the supreme power. But I will give you ,a word of advice. Those ain.ong yua who have enough to support li.fe shoa]d what they can spare in making the incor people happy. They .will then live h. nd well. If you do this, I will take bac:, 1112 rub 3 of the land, and keep it, while you a.v,„.5d the trouble you fear," and thus it waL done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>hWDLES.</head>
        <p>hWDLES.</p>
        <p>Why are little, birds happy in the morn- ing? Because th.eir bills are over dew. Why does a donkey prefer thistles to corn? Because he is an as.</p>
        <p>What is higher and handsomer when the head is off? The pillow. Why is a conceited girl like a music book? Because she is full of air.</p>
        <p>Why would a pelican make a good lawyers Because he knows how to stretch his bill.</p>
        <p>JOKES.</p>
        <p>Stout Lady (at street crossing, to policeman): "Could you see me across the street, officer?" Officer: "Sure, ma'am, I could see ye ten times the distance aisy."</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l would like to become a member of your "Children's Corner," but I do not know the rules. I live at Fleetwood, but my father and mothcr formerly belonged to Rossendale. I am ten years of age. I am send- ing you a piece of poetry, which I wrote my- self. Hoping to see it in print. I remain, yours sincerely, HARGRXAVES PARKINSON.</p>
        <p>OUR NURSERY. (To Uncle Ned.) The north wind doth blow, And we have had snow, And we're all in a terrible fix, For Nurse is away, Writing notes, I daresay ; But she will be back in a trix. And Sis, over there, I have quarrelled with her; So there in the oorner she sits; And Bob's bumped his head (He says he's quits dead), But Tom pushed him off, 'to be quits. And Flo, just by me, Is playing at tea, Though what's there's in that I don't</p>
        <p>know. And poor little Loll Has got a new doll, And won't lend it Kate, "Oh, no, no." And Jim, that, you see, Flays with both Sig and me-, And wants us to make it all up; So I'll go to her, so, And she will not say "No," When I show her my chocolate cup. But it's Nurse that I hear, And to go on I fear, For she's sure to put me off to bed, As I cannot it mend, I will come to the and, So farewell to you, Uncle Ned.</p>
        <p>................M4......0</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am writing to you now. to. send a piece of poetry. Hasn't it been cold and, miserable Wits winter, eepecially when the snow is turned into this dirty slush? I hope by now that your cold is better. Your loving Friend, J. A. KNIGHT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ENGLISH AS SHE IS PRONOUNCED.</head>
        <p>ENGLISH AS SHE IS PRONOUNCED.</p>
        <p>The wind was rough, • And cold and blough, She kept her hands within her mough.. It chill'd her through, Her nose grough blough. And still the squall the faster flough.. And yet, although There was nough enough, The weather was a cruel fough. It made her cough— Pray do not scough— She cough'd until her hat blew ough. Ah, you may laugh. You silly caugh, I'd liltc to beat you with my staugh.</p>
        <p>- „</p>
      </div>
