        <head>' _.'....' . ':-.....-L.--,..:4,1,..,::.4,........:,....,.::7-'?!:.;:-.:-...::',f,.H.''.-..-:.:1:',-;'.:.'ir,;:',.;',..7.",t,':.--.:.•:.:*:if,...i::..-,....:1:-..f.,... .i''.--.'..,.'..'.:'..,j'.-::-:::.!..;.,',. ',-:";:'-';..::'...1L".:'..' ' ' ..,:,,,i,..‘:: –</head>
        <p>' _.'....' . ':-.....-L.--,..:4,1,..,::.4,........:,....,.::7-'?!:.;:-.:-...::',f,.H.''.-..-:.:1:',-;'.:.'ir,;:',.;',..7.",t,':.--.:.•:.:*:if,...i::..-,....:1:-..f.,... .i''.--.'..,.'..'.:'..,j'.-::-:::.!..;.,',. ',-:";:'-';..::'...1L".:'..' ' ' ..,:,,,i,..‘:: -</p>
        <p>ENGLAND'S DANGER.</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield Addresses the Progressive Labour League.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Warrington Progressive Labour League on Tuesday evening, Captain G. R. Crosfield delivered an address on "England's Danger." Mr. T. Barnes presided over a fair attendance. Captain CROSFIELD said he was in sympathy with many of the aspirations of that league and particularly with their desire for an alteration of the land laws. It was very wrong that speculators should be allowed to buy up the land round the cities and hold it for many years, thus causing great congestion in the cities. There were many grave dangers at home but he wanted them to consider dangers from without. He would not mince matters and he said that the danger to this Country and to the Empire at present was Germany. He had no hostile feeling against Germany, on the contrary he had a great admiration Pr that country. But admiration for social developement of the Germans must not blind them: to the hard facts of existence. Here was a new nation which was increasing at the rate of a million men and women per annum, a nation which was avowedly ambitious and which had at her head a great man, who was imbued with the idea of building up a great world-empire like the British Empire. Germany had not got the land requisite for her excess of population, and there was no doubt she would give a great deal to possess some of the fertile spots which we possessed. Germany would want strong, healthy, loyal colonies, and that would be the time when friction might come between the two countries. Germany's navy at present, though only , equal to our channel. and Atlantic fleets, was a very powerful factor, and the Kaiser was rapidly developing it. Germany had an uulimited number of men at her disposal, and with all those forces at her command was it likely she was going to knuckle under when friction , arose? He did not think so. A great many people were thinking the day of war was over, and arbitration was going to settle everything. They had allowed their idealism to run away with their common-sense. While the National Service League was fully in favour of arbitration, he declared that arbitration would succeed best where was a physical force to back it up if necessary They must have a sound citizen army—a sound seconp line of defence to guard our shores,and to give our navy a free hand to prevent it being absolutely hampered and fettered by being tied round our coast. He (Captain Crosfield) was absolutely against the old idea of com- pelling men to fight over-seas ; but every young man should have a short training so that he should be pre- pared of his own accord to defend the Empire. Mr. Haldane was trying to get the people accustomed to the idea of a "nation in arms," and trying to build up a second line of defence on a voluntary basis. He admit- ted Mr. Haldane was doing a lot of good in trying to ' raise the status of the Volunteers, but that was simply tinkering, and was not going to solve the question of ; raising a force sufficiently formidable in numbers' dis- cipline and training to make the Germans think twice before they tried to invade this country. If we went on in our present state of unpreparedness—if anything we were in a worse state now than we were during the Boer war—ruin would come to the Empire just as it had come to Greece, Italy, Spain and other countries. People were growing luxurious and selfish because of long peace and security, which was the mother of danger and the grandmother of destruction. Unless we pre- pared in the next five or ten years by listening to the 1 warning voice of the veteran Field Marshal Earl Roberts he thought we should go the way of the vast empires I he had mentioned. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A discussion folhivrea. Mr. MORTON said it appeared to him that increased physical force merely accenuated the combative element and would mean that when war broke out it would be on a larger and more devastating scale. What was wanted to back up arbitration was a pacific force and that could be built up by industrial organization and the betterment of the working classes. Mr. WYNNE JONES did not think we were in any danger from Germany where there were millions of social democrats who were opposed to the military spirit. Considering the great expenditure on the Army'we should be sufficiently prepared already. He contended that nuiversal training would conduce to a war-like spirit. In his view the time would soon come when universal pace would prevail. Mr. ELWELL supported the proposals of the National Service League as being the best means of ensuring peace. Mr. RANDLES did not think we need fear danger from Germany. 3 Mr.S. WRIGHT shared the same view and also pointed out that nine-tenths of the Labour leaders of England were in favour of peace. There was no evidence that employers would be patriotic enough to keep open the situations of men who underwent the training. The CHAIRMAN thought every man should have a military training but that every consideration should be ' paid to those who served in regard to their employ- ment. He moved a vote of thanks to Captain Crosfleld. Mr. HITCHIN seconded and as an ex-soldier made some remarks reflecting upon the competency of the ogicers in the army. Many, he said, could hardly write their own names. Captain CROSFIELD : I cannot listen to that. It is absolute nonsense. As holding a commission in the army I cannot sit and listen to my brother officers being sla dered in this disgraceful manner. Mr. lirtcHis : I. have pleasure in seconding the vote of thanks. Captain CROSFIELD : I don't wish you to second it. The vote was heartily carried and Captain CROSFILD replied to the various criticisms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist"]</p>
        <p>Warrington have had an eventful time during the past week, and one unfortunately that they cannot look back upon with unmixed satisfaction. They have played three matches and won two of them, but the one they lost was worth the other two put together. Oldham came to Warrington on Saturday, and did what no other team has done this season, by defeating the locals on their own patch. There was nothing half-hearted about the defeat either, for Oldham amassed 14 points against Warrington's 5, and were value for all they got. For once in a way, the War- rington men had a decided "off day," and as this occurred in a match of vital importance the chagrin of the crowd was intense. Warrington were expected to win and ought to have won. if they had they would have been second in the League instead of sixth, and one of the coveted top places would have been almost secure.</p>
        <p>The position of affairs is not at all favourable to Warrington, though they have a reasonable chance if they pull themselves together. We can leave Halifax out of the reckoning—they are safe as leaders. Leeds have to play Hunslet at home, and Wigan, Dewsbury, Hull Kingston Rovers, Broughton Rangers and Warrington away. The "Wirepullers " ought to pass them in the race. Oldham have Wakefield, Bradford and Halifax at home, and Wakefield, Leigh, Hull and Wigan away. Warrington have Broughton, Halifax, Rochdale and Leeds and at home Hu dderstield,Halifax, Swinton, and Runcorn away. The safest clubs are Halifax and Runcorn, while Oldham,Warrington and Wigan are all in the running for the other two vacancies. In all probability the sti uggle still has to come between Oldham and Warrington,for Wigan are better placed than either.</p>
        <p>The League Table up-to-date is as follows : Per-</p>
        <p>Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P.centage. Halifax 27 23 2 2 99 97 489 32 41 187 88.88 Leeds 24 17 5 2 62 80 364 27 43 183 75.00 Runcorn 93 17 6 0 62106 442 31 39 179 7391 Oldimm 27 19 7 1 62 80 36437 45 209 72.22 Wigan 27 19 7 1 85131 563 42 49 231 72.22 Warrington 26 18 7 1 62116 472 41 46 220 71.08 Keighley —2O 13 6 1 53 72 322 34 43 197 67.50 Hunslet 96 17 9 1/ 70 88 404 52 60 284 65.38 Broughton 25 15 9 1 67102 440 32 40 190 6200. Salford 97 16 18 0 72 97 435 58 53 275 59.25 Widnes . l6 7 8 1 32 21 145 36 58 246 46.28 Hull K.RI: 25 12 13 0 43 63 275 46 55 257 48.00 Barrow 94 11 12 1 38'73 295 52 76 332 47.91 Swinton 25 11 14 0 46 58 266 47 66 292 44.00 Dewsbury 23 913 1 44 70 298 54 78 342 41.30 Batley 21 812 1 44 42 214 45 64 282. 40.47 Roohdale 91 812 1 28 65 251 30 59 237 4037 Leigh 91 914 1 37 69 281 50 59 277 39.58 Hud'sfield 23 9 14, 0 41 83 331 67 81 377 39.13 Hull 26 10 16 0 44 60 268 69 101 431 3846 Bradford _26 915 2 59 80 358 55 74 332 38.24 Walefild T 2O 613 1 28 43 185 43 80 327 32.50 St. Helens 20 614 0 40 55 245 39 70 288 30.00 York l7 413 0 23 36 154 41 66 280 2352 Bramley lB 116 1 91769 49 L 2 374 8.33 Liverpl. C 26 025 1 10 15 65 148 2741118 2•00 The Pontefract Club, having disbanded, their record has been withdrawn from the above table.</p>
        <p>If Warrington had beaten Oldham, as they should hq.ve done, their percentage would have been 75.00, and they would have just. displaced Leeds on scoring average. The latter go to Wigan to-day, and Halifax to Salford, so that Warrington's position would prob- ably have been strengthened.</p>
        <p>To revert to the Oldham match, I think the visitors were just as much above form as Warrington were below it. They played fine, resolute football and adapted themselves better to the windy conditions than Warringion. The home forwards were capital in the scrums and worked really hard, but the backs without either Isherwood or Fish were totally dis- organised. Taylor was quite out cf condition and could not raise a gallop, and his weakness seemed to put Dickenson off as well. Jenkins was the only back to really maintain his reputation, and on Satur- day he was streets ahead of Lees. The outstanding player on the Oldham side was Cash who gave War- rington a lot of trouble and was responsible for two of the tries. Brooks could not quite keep him in check. It should be added that Oldham had all the luck there was about on Saturday, but they deserved their unexpected victory.</p>
        <p>---t On Monday, Warrington took it out of Liverpool City to the tune of 52 points to nil. In the first half they scored 10 tries and 2 goals ; in the second half, six trips and no goals. In fact the goal-kicking was scandalous. The combination was little better, and Warrington's physique h.7.clas much to do with the big score as their cleverness,which was seldom manifested. Fish turned out again, and that is about all. lie kept Harry Foster company at full back most of the time and still carries about a ton too much weight.</p>
        <p>The third match came off at Widnes on Thursday. As a change from the Liverpool burlesque it was quite lively for the " Chemicals " did not dissolve very readily. Why they chose to play against the wind in the first half is a mystery, for it resulted in Warring- ton gettine, a useful lead of 10 points. Widnes could only nib Tour of these off 'when their turn came, and Warrington settled the issue by a third try near the close. All three were scored by the forwards, Boardman getting two and Naylor one. Both packs were lively, but Warrington haa an advantage in weight end they used it. •</p>
        <p>There is no doubt at all that the Warington men are playing a long way below form. Even against Liverpool, the combination of the backs- was very poor, and at 'Widnes on Thursday it was again unequal to obtaining points. The attack seems quite dis- organised and the men are not handling the ball at all well. Individually they are as relioble as ever, and on Thursday proved too clever for Widnes in the second half when defensive Measures were imperative. Once more Jenkins was the star performer, Isherwood and Dickinson not showing the best judgment on the attack. Berry, too, was only moderate, and good luck coupled with hesitancy by the home backs, saved Warrington ourtwo' occasions when the full back was beaten.</p>
        <p>This match counted in the South-West Lancashire Competition also, which now stands as follows : Pts.</p>
