        <p>GENERAL BULLER AT WARRINGTONs</p>
        <p>Colonel Rrpow.tir seconded. "The statue," he said " is a speaking likeness of our friend Colonel MacCarthy O'Leary, and I feel that if those gallant men whose names are on that tablet are with us to-day in spirit, they will think that he is the right man to point the way. We in Warrington admired very much Colonel O'Leary. He was well known to us as he was for five years adjutant to our Volunteers here, during which time at least a thousand of the young men of the town passed through his hands. We therefore take an extra interest in him and feel what a friend we have lost. I feel sure that they and those his comrades who come afterwards to view this— and perhaps they may bring their children—will point to the statue and say with pride : He was the man we served under and admired and respected." (Applause.) The vote was enthusiastically carried, rousing cheers being given for Sir Redvers and Lady Buller. Sir REDvEks BuLcErt, in reply assured the gathering that no compliment could be so dear to him than to be asked to unveil the memerial to those comrades and especially to the gallant Colonel. " Anyone who has served in the South Lmcashire Regiment " he con- tinued "knows that he has served with good and true men. I have always heard that there is a tradition in Warrington that Warrington was a Ring's City. Certainly the South Lancashire men who served with me proved that they were King's men to the backbone. It was a great gr itiflcation to me to have the honour of having them under my command and I can only say I am grateful to the South Lancashire Regiment for having brought me to-day to Warrington and to all the kin-' people of Warrington who have so well received me." (Anplause.) This concluded the ceremony. Sir Redvers Buller then inspected the guard of honour and troops. After- wards there was a pleasing incident,the General inspect- ing a large contingent of veterans and local active service men and addressing a few kind words to each. . . .</p>
        <p>UNVEILING OF THE 'SOUTH LANCASHIRE WAR MEMORIAL.</p>
        <p>Interesting eeremonies and Speeches.</p>
        <p>a I. 4.</p>
        <p>The soldiers then marched from the Gardens and the General was escorted through cheering multitudes along Sankey-street and Bridge-street and Wilderspool-road to Stockton Heath. Thus ended a memorable day for Warrington.</p>
        <p>F</p>
        <p>Gregory &amp; Co., Strand.]</p>
        <p>GENERAL SIR REDVERS BULLER, V.C.</p>
        <p>Warrington had on Thursday the honour and pleasure of extending a heartfelt welcome to General Sir Redvers Buller, V.C., who visited our town tc unveil the noble memorial to the soldiers of South Lancashire who fell in the South African campaign. The sun shone approvingly, dispelling the fears which had seen raised by the previous day's boisterous wintry weather. It was indeed cause for congratula- tion that the day which had been so long looked forward to should be favoured with beautifully fine weather, the only drawback being a chill breeze. Early the town began to put on its festive garb, the Town Hall setting a splendid example. Here the decorations were on a lavish and most tasteful scale. From Venetian masts long lines of streamers fluttered gaily, while the building itself had choice embellishments of flags and coats of arms. On the roof there was another fine display of streamers, while the steps and balcony looked a mass of colour in their drapery of Union Jacks and various bright materials. Plants were also well arranged, and the general effect was imposing. Near the Park gates were more Venetian masts with _their long rows of many-coloured flags, and it was generally agreed that the decorations, which had been carried out under the superintendence of the Borough Surveyor (Mr. T. Longdin) were quite in keeping with the reception of such a distinguished soldier. The main streets lost their usual workaday appearance, flags and Patriotic emblems being seen on every hand. From the military point of view, the day's ceremony had a particular significance, and as was only to be expected, the town was invaded with soldiers. The men of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment are at present on service in India, but there was a strong muster of the 2nd Battalion, 350 men accompanied by their fine band, journeying from the South of England to form a guard of honour. Then a con- tingent of Volunteers, with band, came over from St. Helens, and the Warrington Volunteers also furnished a large detachment along with the band of the battalion to join in the ceremonial. About noon, the streets became thronged with people anxious to greet the gallant General. All the schools and many works and places of business closed at noon and the concourse of spectators was also swelled by hundreds of visitors from other towns. Sir Redvers Buller had spent Wednesday evening at High Legh Hall as the guest of Colonel Legh, and " THE KING." on Thursday morning he motored to Warrinaton I accompanied by Colonel and Mrs. Legh. A The CHAIRMAN proposed the toast of "His Majesty Warrington Bridge at 12.45, the part entered at • • , t I the Kin,' "—a toast which, hi; said, would find not only a ready but an affectionate response from all. The landau and, escorted by the Mounted Infiayntry of the peodplre of l thesee ohad t tee ggrreaaatjaarti v iclaanset?tfu living Warrington Volunteers, drove along Bridge-street and i l‘lilianearch of our Kingship No predecessor of his had so Sankey-street to the Town Hall, the General being I great a regard for the liberties and rights of his people; welcome with hearty cheers which he repeatedly : their joys and sorrows always touched a responsive acknowledged. It was somewhat difficult to realize chord in his breast. ,Amongst our statesmen he took the that the kindly-looking gentleman, in civilian attire, : chief place ; as a diplomat he had no peer. Amongst the counsels of the nations of Europe when, as some- was the world-famous hero of many a fight, but the crowds were not slow to recognize the features which times occurred, racial, commercial always politiql differ- ences have long been made familiar by photographs every , always arose,_ his mediation Rwos bserts)tou leltchoamtewhedn wayl beadle Bu t.i (1%1 • . g. citizen and the welcome had a true warm-hearted the history of our times came to be written, among Lancashire ring. i King Edward's many kingly attributes, the ore by which. Ihe would be known the bes