      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>AT STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>General Buller Plants a Tree. The " Heathens " were not a little proud to think that General Buller would honour them with a visit at the conclusion of the ceremony at Warrington, and long before the distinguished soldier was to be expected they turned out in full force to welcome him. Victoria Square and the main streets of the village wore a holiday garb, flags and bunting fluttering gaily in the breeze and sunshine, and the greatest enthusiasm was displayed. The proceedings were a triumph for the tree planters who in their zeal for the cause had ventured to approach Sir Redvers and win his consent to plant a tree, and for once,at any rate, their efforts received unlimited com- mendation from all sections of the commnnity. The appearance of the General with h s mounted escort was the sigaal for an outburst of cheering, which was increased as he, accompanied by Colonel and Mrs.Legh, descended from his carriage to make the acquaintance of the village fathers. The ceremony which followed was a brief but pleasing one. Mr. Plinston, a promi- nent member of tne Tree Planting Committee, welcomed the General in the name of the village, and in asking him to plant the tree, he explained the scheme which they advocated, the beautifying of towns and villages, and added that whilst in their first season they had planted 100 trees, they had this year embarked upon a perhaps even more ambitious scheme—the planting of shrubs &amp;c., on the banks of the Manchester Ship Canal, , in the hope that they would make a picture which would attract many thousands from Warrington and the dis- trict.</p>
        <p>The enthusiasm of the populace was increased as Sir Redvers in a business-like way performed the cere- mony and the proposing of vote of thanks, by Dr. PARKER was greeted with hearty cheers. "The General has planted an oak" said Dr. Parker" a British oak—(cheers)—whose sturdy growth and imperishable texture is an emblem I trust,for ever,of the stability of the British race and constitution. Honour has been done to Stockton Heath and I ask you to show your appreciation by three hearty cheers." The request was promptly responded to. General BULLER said in reply : "I can assure you it gives me great pleasure to have stopped at Stockton Heath. frecollect that in one of Sir Walter Scott's novels the old Laird advises his son to be always plant- ing trees as they would be growing while he slept. That was useful advice and you cannot do better than follow it. I am glad that W 3 now have local government which enables the people to beautify the district and houses in which they live."</p>
        <p>There was anothar outburst of cheering as Miss Pattie Woodcock on behalf of the committee presented a beautiful bouquet to Mrs. Legh. This terminated the ceremony and after the General had shaken bands with as many as could get within reach, he entered a motor car which was waiting and resumed his journey to High Legh Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Hearty Reception at Lymm.</head>
        <p>Hearty Reception at Lymm.</p>
        <p>A cordial welcome was extended to General Sir Redvers Buller, on his arrival at Lymm Station. on Wednesday evening, when he was met by Colonel Legh, of High Legh Hall. A large crowd assembled on the station to welcome the gallant general, and outside there was a large gathering of the inhabitants. General Buller was received in the waiting-room of the station by the members of the Lymm Tradesmen's Association with hearty cheers, and was presented with an illumin- ated address by Mr. Rogerson chairman of the associa- tion. General Buller in a few words, acknowledged the very pleasing welcome which had been extended to him, and subsequently inspected a number of veterans who had assembled outside the station. Afterwards the General proceeded through the village, which was gaily decorated, to High Legh Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Requiem Mass at St. Mary's Church.</head>
        <p>Requiem Mass at St. Mary's Church.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning a Solemn Requiem Mass for the repose of the souls of Colonel O'Leary and the men of the South Lan. ?ire Regiment who fell in the South African War 7 • elebrated at St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-street. 'here was a large congregation, including the Mayor and a considerable number of members of the Town Council and a contingent of soldiers from Orford Barracks with their officers. Two sons of the late Colonel O'Leary were also present. The Rev. Father Wilson was the celebrant and he was assisted by the Revs. Father Dawes and Father Rylance. After the Gospel Father Wilson addressed the congregation and paid a high tribute to Colonel O'Leary and his fallen comrades.</p>
        <p>At the close of the service the "Last Post " was sounded by the buglers of the South Lancashire Regi- ment, and as the people left the church, it was again sounded from the tower of the building. Mr. Eckersley presided at the organ, and at the beginning of the ser- vice played Guilimant's Dead March, and at the close the Dead March in" Saul."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>South Lancashires Entertainad.</head>
        <p>South Lancashires Entertainad.</p>
        <p>Last evening the members of the 2nd Battalion, South Lancashire Regiment, were entertained to dinner by the Mayor and townspeople. About 300 sat down to an excellent repast which was followed by a smoking concert. Selections were given by the band of the Regiment and the band of the local volunteers the People's College children, Lyric Quartette Party, Misses Broadbent and Wallace, Mr. and Mrs. Hannan, Messrs. Willet, T. Humphreys, and J. Emson, and Master Lawton. Mr. W. H. Payton was the accomp.inist. The toast of the 2nd Battalion was drunk on the proposition of the MAYOR, and responded to by Colonel ADAM and Mr. H. ROBERTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Crush at Queen's Gardens</head>
        <p>The Crush at Queen's Gardens</p>
        <p>To THE EDITOR, "EXAMINER." Dear Sir.—Will you kindly allow me to tender my regrets.to those subscribers (ladies and gentlemen) who were compelled on Thursday to wait so long a time for admission to the Queen's Gardens. Unfortunately by some oversight this matter was not dealt with according to our instructions and I am sure my Committee would agree with me in expressing the deep regret we all feel that any of our kind friends should have been so much inconvenienced by what, so far as Tam aware was the only untoward circumstance in what was other- wise a most successful function.</p>
        <p>Again expressing my regret for this mistake which, considering the crowded condition of the street under the circumstances was almost unavoidable.—l beg to remain, HENRY ROBERTS. _</p>
        <p>—, Chairman Executive Soldiers' Memorial Committee.</p>
