any a famous victory. The statue, of assault on the last Boer fortress between us and Lady- ' smith. I may mention incidentally that it was always, Course, was the main object of attention. As yet, a when commanding troops, my desire and ambition that I sheet concealed it from view, and at the foot had been every regiment should have its share of the fighting and erected a platform for the chief participants in the the brigade to which the South Lancashires belonged ceremony. The entrance of Sir Redvers Buller was had not up to that any been very lucky. Consequently the signal for a general salute from the troops and a I had brought them over from Chievley to take a lead- great outburst of cheering from the people. Sir Redvers ing part in the assault on Pieter's Hill. The regiment at once mounted the platform, being accompanied by had marched over and they were lying down to rest in and Mayoress, Mr. H. Roberts, Colonel and a column of companies and I rode up to Colonel O'Leary the Mayor anl who came forward to meet me. I said" Well, Colonel, Mrs. Legh. Viscountess Ikerrin, Colonel Adam, Colonel and how are the South Lancashires " "The South Ridgway, Mr. Alfred Drury (sculptor) and Mr. J. Lyon Lancashires," he replied, " are right." I rode round the Whittle. battalion after him and I thought they looked right and ; Mr. H. ROBERTS said: Sir Redvers Buller. A very a very few hours afterwards pleasant and agreeable duty has been allotted to me— THEY PROVED THAT THEY WERE RIGHT, i to ask you,, on behalf of the subscribers, to unveil our (Applause.) They did under his lead what up to then soldiers' memorial. When the Executive Committee had been considered to be practically an impossibilty. decided to invite you to visit our town for this purpose They attacked in front, fortifications held by very deter-' they had in mind not only the long and distinguished mined men. No doubt they were helped by guns; it services which you have rendered to our King and would have been a