was nothing; a spent bullet knocked the pencil out of what in the soldier is called heroism and what in the my hand." (Laughter.) It is people who do not and private man is very often much too little regarded. It will not think who believe that war is an occasional is called self-sacrifice, appreciation of duty and it is very bloody battle. There is very much more in war called by all sorts and various names, but nevertheless than that, and the South Lancashires while they were it is the same thing—it is heroism and that is the one keeping open the communications, fed not only the factor in life which alone, I think, can really teach us 40,000 men of the Natal army but fed also the whole to end our lives happily. I often recollect at the of the troops Lord Roberts had with him on the other unveiling of these memorials, a sentence in a great side, because his communications were not kept open. I writer who describes the end of an old man dying corn- have no doubt it was at that time that the South Lance- fortably in his bed, surrounded by his children and his shire Volunteers earned for the "old fortieth" the children's children, and dying in complete happiness name we have just heard : "The regiment that never and safety, and he says that is a beautiful picture to slept. (Applause.) Therefore you must see that look upon. But there is another more beautiful than although your Volunteers had not the good luck to get that. It is the death that comes after a life passed in the good chance of a fight, they hardship and in sorrow, a life along a rough, thorny, HAD MOST OF THE HARD KNOCKS. and dangerous path, with the grave gaping to receive I think it is only fair I should say that here, because it it before the victory is won. Such a life is really the seems as if in the future we are about to call for the precursor of a beautiful death and of such a life the reserves of our fighting army, upon those men whcm true symbol is the Cross. Let us remember, that this is</p>
        <p>not only a memorial to a man and to gallant soldiers, but it is a memorial embodying a really true and valuable instruction to everybody who passes it—that in our life, whatever danger or difficulty or morel sin confronts us, we ought to meet it end fight it to the death. Mr. Mayor, I hand this memorial to your keep- ing. (Applause.) The MAYOR said : It gives me great pleasure to accept on behalf of this County Borough the very noble monument which has been erected by subscriptions to the memory of the officers and mu of the South Lancashire Regiment who gave their lives for their country during the recent South African war. It seems to me very fitting that such memorials should be erected, for while we ourselves can very well remember the enthusiastic scenes which accompanied the departure of our brave Lancashire lads to Sonth Africa,tlaere are generations to come after us to whom this monument will over speak of the great sacrifices that wore made and the never-to-be forgotten patriotism that was evidenced when through England the call to arms MIS hear ; and every village and every town sent its sons to do battle for their country's honour. Very many of thorn in common with those whose honoured memory we now commemorate sleep far out on the veldt. Their deeds can nv3 rbe forgotten Not once nor twice in our roa,gh island story The path of duty was the road to glory. (Applause). I have very great pleasure in accepting this monument on behalf of the inhabitants of the county borough of Warrington. (Applause). Colonel ADAM then moved a hearty vote of thanks to Sir Redvers Buller for his very great kindness in unveiling the memorial. "Idoso " he said "specially on behalf of my own beloved regiment with which I have had the honour of being associated for over twenty- seven years, and I say from the heart of every man in the regiment, that no greater compliment could have been paid to our deal than that Sir Redvers Buller should unveil this memorial. (Applause). There is a peculiar touch of human nature that makes the whole world kin in the respect that is paid to ow dead. We value no sympathy and no respect more deeply then we do that final tribute, and I am sure I am only voicing your sentiments when 1 say we feel most deeply thankful to Sir Redvers fof his great kindness."</p>
