      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>HOW COLONEL O'LEARY WAS KILLED.</head>
        <p>HOW COLONEL O'LEARY WAS KILLED.</p>
        <p>It was at the decisive battle of Pieter's Hill that Colonel O'Leary fell whilst leading his men to victory A graphic description of the engagement is given by one who took part in it, T. Neligan, of the South Lancashire Regiment, in his book "From Preston to Ladysmith." He says :—" Onward the men pressed, taking advantage of all cover until the summit of the hill had been reached ; then, as if by mutual consent, both officers and men rested for a brief space of time to regain their l2reath, knowing that in another hour's • time the battle would be decided. By this time,about 4 p.m., the grass within three hundred yards of the enemy's position had caught fire from the blazing of the lyddite shells, and the khaki-clad figures showed up most prominently against the dark and sombre background. When about two hundred and fifty yards from the main position the firing was terrible, and the bullets came so thick that my regiment attempted to take cover behind a small rise in the ground, but this proved to be quite inadequate for the purpose. Seeing this, the commanding officer gave the order to' fix bayonets and charge,' saying at the time, 'Remember, men, the eyes of Lancashire are watching you to-day!' At his words the whole regiment rose like one man, and the black slope fairly twinkled with the glitter of the bayonets as they flashed in the sun. Like a wall of rock the gallant 40th closed upon their foe, and the best dis- ciplined troops in the wide world could not have with- stood that irresistible rush. Like demons let loose they charged upon the enemy, who quailed before the contact, and those who were able rushed precipitately The'a%va y falling B, ue rs i dozens who n wers u under unable eeto telhe akeb ifee of our eseape men. suede piteously for quarter, and the Lancashire lads, now though ugh v lh bud grace, u mercifulit as they rhsacd been t hy w brave,e burning t rtuiiolg them,tu h avenge the losses they had already sustained. The colonel fell shortly after he gave the order to charge, whilst leading his regiment on, and Major Hall, the second in command, took charge of the regiment and gallantly conducted the charge unto the end."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-02-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Cadets in the Guard of Honour.</head>
        <p>Cadets in the Guard of Honour.</p>
        <p>Part of the Guard of Honour to General Buller in Queen Gardens was composed of a detachment of 50 boys with bugle band, who belong to the Ist Volunteer Cadet Corps (East Lancashire Regiment),and are pupils at Stonyhurst College, Blackburn. It will perhaps be interesting to our readers to know that the late Lieut.- Colonel MacCarthy O'LeAry was educated at Stonyhurst College, and before him, his rather, whilst at the pre- sent time a son of the former is a student there, and another son has just left to take a Commission in the Army.</p>
      </div>
