        <p>of their principal, Mr. Robson, of Mr. Albery and Miss Frost, his chief assistant, and of the other members of the staff of the institute. (Applause). Summing up, M.r. Jolley said that tip general result was an increased demand on the raLbs of, in round figures, 21 in the 2. But for the delay in the opening of the Beamont School, id. would have sufficed; as it was, not year would of course benefit. Mr. Jolley then laid before the committee some information mainly derived from . the recently issued report of a Departmental Committee, bearing on the cost of education in England and Wales and on the great variations, both real and apparent, in the burden on the rates in different areas. The figures, he contended that the Warrington Committee came out aniongst the most economical in the conntry.He did not suggest that the whole credit for the efficiency of our educational administration and for the satisfactory state of our educational finance was exclusivey due to the members of the committee or any section or them. 'He wanted to say as plainly and as clearly as possible I that both the committee and the whole community were I greatly indebted to their Director and to their Treasurer for the admirable manner in which the work of their respective departments were performed,and for the firm- ness and courtesy with which they discharged their responsible and not always pleasant duties. (Hear,hear, and applause.) PEOPLE'S COLLEGE.—ITS FUTURE NAME. It is reported to ~ the School Management Committee that their resolution in reference to the tittle of the Warrington British School had not been approved by the Education Com- mittee at their meeting on the 21st Januaay, 1907, and, after the communication which had been received from the managers upon the subject had been read and the matter further discussed; it %yea resolved "That it be suggested to the Board of Education that the Warring- ton British School be in future called the Warrington Breiatnall School." The following letter on this subject was read from Mr. C. Jackaon Holmes, the corresponding secretary of People's College : "As the managers of the school are given to understand that the School Management Com- mittee are not disposed to acquiesce in the suggestion made by this °Committee, narnely to call the schools 'The Warrington Arpley-street Schools,' they desire respect- fully to urge you to give the matter further considera- tiod, as there is the strongest possible objection to any personal names being acceptable on the general ground that they are apt to be meaningless in time, and are always open to the objection of having a political or denominational significance. There can be no question as to the advisability of accepting a name by which the schools are already so well-known, and which haslthe great advantage of being descriptive of position." Canon STEVENSON said he had not the slightest interest in the selection of the name, and be did not mean to vote for one side or the other. Councillor FULTON-SMITH seconded the passing of the minutes. Alderman TINNION proposed as an amendment that the minute relating to the name of the school be not ;</p>
        <p>schools by personal names they 'should keep green the memory of a man who had laid the town under a per- manent debt of obligation. Mr. PLINSTON, the Rev. J. Yonge and Mr. Burgess supported the amendment, which on being pat to the vote was carried by a large majority. BOLTON SCiiOOL CONTRACT. It was recommended by the Sites and Buildings Com- mittee (a) that the tender of Mr. Harry Fairclough, amounting to £8,062, for the erection of the" Bolton" Council School, be accepted ; and (b) that application be made to the Local Government Board for their sanc- tion to the raising of a loan to cover the cost of the erection and tarnishing of the building. The DEPUTY-MA.YOR moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said they were more interesting than usual because they had taken one more step to get a new school. By changing the plans they had managed to save .C 2,000 or £37000, and he believed the school would be as good when finished. Mr. WALKER. seconded. I Mr. PLINSTON said the tender was not quite the lowest, but he supported the passing of the minute because the local man should have a contract if the difference in cost was not too great. He supposed the condition as to the fair wage clause was all right and also the question of the bricksetters' labourers. The matter was still open, and he hoped it would be considered by the I committee. I Councillor BALL said that in the case of the brick- setters' labourers all they contended WES that a proper remuneration should be paid for work done. Sixpence an hour was not to be considered excessiye. Mr. ROBERTS: I understood the object of Mr. Plinston was to secure a fair rate of wages in the schools. Do you suggest it was not paid in the other school? Mr. PLINSTON : Just so. Mr. ROBERTS Why then did not all the unionist joiners and bricksetters strike? M.. PLLNSTON : lam not allowed to answer that. Mr. ROBERTS said he believed that when the truth , came to be known it would be found there had been an ; absolute misconception as to facts, and it was only fair I to the contractor for the building of the " Beamont " I Schools to state that so far as the Builders' Federation</p>
